WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:05.690
Michael: That makes me feel just great.

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:08.750
I really thank you very much.

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:13.990
One of the reasons I volunteered to do this
introduction is because of my admiration and

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:17.570
my respect for the person I'm about to introduce.

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:21.430
I'm not going to tell you anything about Leonard
Nimoy, the actor.

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:23.660
I think you all know something about that.

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:28.930
What I am going to tell you are some things
that you may not know; that in addition to

00:00:28.930 --> 00:00:31.789
his acting, Mr. Nimoy is an author and a poet.

00:00:31.789 --> 00:00:40.059
He's the author of 6 books, including 5 books
of poetry, books of his photograph.

00:00:40.059 --> 00:00:43.190
He's a professional photographer as well.

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:50.149
You also may have missed his hit album which
was entitled "Leonard Nimoy Sings Mr. Spock's

00:00:50.149 --> 00:00:59.300
Music From Outer Space" which sold over 130,000
copies, so he's also a musician.

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:04.820
He's also, aside from Star Trek which everybody
knows about, a very distinguished stage actor

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:07.100
who's done very well as Sherlock Holmes.

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:15.170
He had a 1-man show that was very widely respected,
a show on Vincent, a show about a different

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:17.140
type of hero.

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:23.060
Leonard Nimoy is also, and this was most surprising
to me at all, a grandfather.

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:25.160
He has a 3 and 1/2-year-old grandchild.

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:28.479
He has 2 children in their 30s.

00:01:28.479 --> 00:01:34.939
I should tell you that the buzz in Hollywood
is and has been on Leonard Nimoy for some

00:01:34.939 --> 00:01:43.690
years, that when it comes to someone who gives
his time to a variety of charities, goes out

00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:53.189
of his way to help people, is a gentleman
in his work, Mr. Nimoy is exemplar.

00:01:53.189 --> 00:01:58.860
He has the kind of reputation that every actor
or actress or every director would like to

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:02.880
have but very few of them do.

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:07.509
Most importantly of all, and I think the main
reason that Mr. Nimoy is with us tonight,

00:02:07.509 --> 00:02:14.790
is because he is, aside from his acting background,
an outstanding filmmaker.

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:19.950
Just look at his last 3 films and his range
as a director.

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:24.450
Star Trek 4: The Voyage Home for which we
just saw the trailer is, in my humble opinion

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:25.599
… and look, I'm a movie critic.

00:02:25.599 --> 00:02:26.599
I have to have opinions.

00:02:26.599 --> 00:02:28.069
I have to give them to you, right?

00:02:28.069 --> 00:02:33.950
Star Trek 4: The Voyage Home is by far the
best Star Trek of the series and one of the

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:39.239
best science fiction adventure movies, I think,
that has come out in this decade.

00:02:39.239 --> 00:02:44.140
Then he made Three Men and a Baby, which is
one of the most commercially successful films

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:48.569
and a completely different type of film, very
few special effects … unless you include

00:02:48.569 --> 00:02:52.330
the baby urinating as a special effect…

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:59.019
very few special effects and yet a charming
comedy, a delightful comedy, that was a huge

00:02:59.019 --> 00:03:02.739
smash hit, the most successful movie of 1987.

00:03:02.739 --> 00:03:08.440
Then his most recent film which is a film
for which I feel a particular passion, is

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:10.400
a film called The Good Mother.

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:13.580
I will tell you that long before I knew that
I was going to have the privilege of sharing

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:19.430
this podium with Mr. Nimoy here at Hillsdale,
I was very proud to list The Good Mother on

00:03:19.430 --> 00:03:22.950
my Best of Year List for 1988.

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:29.370
It's a film that deserved a much larger audience
than it received, a film that is as different

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:35.019
from Star Trek 4: The Voyage Home as that
film is from Three Men as a Baby.

00:03:35.019 --> 00:03:39.410
Here's a man as a director who has shown that
he can direct science fiction adventure, an

00:03:39.410 --> 00:03:45.890
action, who has shown that he can direct light
comedy and has shown a tremendous facility

00:03:45.890 --> 00:03:52.379
for directing a drama that raises some of
the most important issues of love and parenting

00:03:52.379 --> 00:03:58.660
and some of the difficulties of a single mother
in dealing with sexuality, and with that sexuality

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:02.190
potentially interfering with her role as a
mother.

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:05.489
I was very proud of the fact that … Once
again, long before I knew that I was going

00:04:05.489 --> 00:04:10.750
to be appearing with Mr. Nimoy here at Hillsdale,
I said on the air that I thought that Diane

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:14.360
Keaton's performance under Leonard Nimoy's
direction in The Good Mother was the best

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:18.419
performance that any actress gave in 1988.

00:04:18.419 --> 00:04:19.760
I still believe that.

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:23.530
I think it's a shame she was not nominated
for an Academy Award and, frankly, it's a

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:27.220
shame that Mr. Nimoy wasn't nominated for
the Academy Award he deserved as one of the

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:29.280
best directors of the year.

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:35.300
Having said that, we have lots to talk about
concerning his films, the issues that they

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:41.050
raised, and the entire question of Hollywood
and its role within the popular culture.

00:04:41.050 --> 00:04:47.120
To proceed to those questions, it is a very
great honor to introduce a multitalented and

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:51.330
very gracious gentleman, Mr. Leonard Nimoy.

00:04:51.330 --> 00:05:11.229
Michael: You're supposed to take this chair
right here.

00:05:11.229 --> 00:05:17.870
Leonard: Thank you.

00:05:17.870 --> 00:05:22.289
Thank you.

00:05:22.289 --> 00:05:26.340
Michael: I think this counts as a friendly
crowd.

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:28.020
Leonard: I would say so.

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:29.020
Yeah.

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:34.710
Michael: Let me begin, if I may, at a difficult
point, which is I know that The Good Mother

00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:40.000
is just out on video now and is doing very
well on video and you … It's number 10 apparently

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:42.880
on the Los Angeles Times Video Chart.

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:47.070
When it opened in theaters, it was not a commercial
success.

00:05:47.070 --> 00:05:48.070
Leonard: No.

00:05:48.070 --> 00:05:49.070
True.

00:05:49.070 --> 00:05:54.650
Michael: One of the things that I think some
critics, not this critic, complained about

00:05:54.650 --> 00:06:00.250
in the film was the fact that despite that
trailer which is somewhat misleading, you

00:06:00.250 --> 00:06:07.190
didn't seem to really take a side on behalf
of the Diane Keaton character who was fighting

00:06:07.190 --> 00:06:12.280
for the custody of her child that you seem
to leave some of the questions open.

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:17.069
The question that I would have is would you
say that that was an accurate description

00:06:17.069 --> 00:06:19.850
of what you were doing in the film?

00:06:19.850 --> 00:06:25.840
Having said that, would you do anything differently
if you have the film to direct again?

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:33.060
Leonard: Let me say that the audience response
or the lack of audience for the film was a

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:42.280
major disappointment, a surprise to many of
us including the marketing people at Disney.

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:46.900
When we were making the film, it was understood
that it would be a film that had to be nurtured

00:06:46.900 --> 00:06:52.270
in the marketplace, that we … you couldn't
just toss it out there in several hundred

00:06:52.270 --> 00:06:57.900
theaters and expect people to flock to see
it because it's somewhat an unusual film.

00:06:57.900 --> 00:07:04.320
We were hoping for critical and audience reaction
to build gradually to an awareness of the

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:07.270
picture and that would hopefully draw in audience.

00:07:07.270 --> 00:07:13.060
By the time the picture was ready for release,
the Disney marketing people were convinced

00:07:13.060 --> 00:07:18.539
that this procedure was not necessary, that
there was enough anticipation for the film,

00:07:18.539 --> 00:07:24.509
enough of a “want to see” quotient, for
the picture, that they could as they say broad

00:07:24.509 --> 00:07:30.889
and open into something like 800 or 900 theaters,
and we're … we hit a disaster.

00:07:30.889 --> 00:07:33.060
We hit a brick wall when we opened it.

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:39.650
I think the first weekend we did maybe a third,
25% of the business they had hoped to do.

00:07:39.650 --> 00:07:42.520
By the end of the first 10 days, it was obvious
the film was finished and was coming out of

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:45.050
the theater.

00:07:45.050 --> 00:07:54.910
I am still today reviewing some of the decisions
we made including content, the way the film

00:07:54.910 --> 00:07:58.570
was made, including what's in the movie.

00:07:58.570 --> 00:08:06.979
It would probably be easy and comfortable
in the hindsight to say now that I might want

00:08:06.979 --> 00:08:15.460
to change some things for the sake of satisfying
audience, but I know … I really don't think

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:16.460
I would.

00:08:16.460 --> 00:08:17.460
I don't think I would.

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:26.630
I set out to make the picture in a way which
would give an audience an opportunity to respond

00:08:26.630 --> 00:08:32.539
to it based on their own prejudices, if you
will, and I think that that is still operative.

00:08:32.539 --> 00:08:33.539
I think the picture does that.

00:08:33.539 --> 00:08:40.110
The picture is kind of a Rorschach test, in
a way, and it has to do with each individual

00:08:40.110 --> 00:08:46.240
member of the audience's feelings about what
is proper, what is correct, what is morally

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:49.781
right, what is morally wrong, ethically right,
ethically wrong in raising children and in

00:08:49.781 --> 00:08:52.690
making decisions about parenting and family.

00:08:52.690 --> 00:09:01.260
These are very, very sensitive issues and
I … What I'm surprised to find, what surprised

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:07.740
me, is that there is that do people come out
in discuss from their own points of view what

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:12.580
happened in the film and what … how they
feel about that and on both sides of the issue,

00:09:12.580 --> 00:09:16.050
that feeling that either that justice was
done or justice was not done.

00:09:16.050 --> 00:09:18.330
That we expected.

00:09:18.330 --> 00:09:24.720
What happens is that if they … I find that
if the film doesn't satisfy individual needs

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:29.810
in terms of the way they felt the story should
go, they're not angry with the society that

00:09:29.810 --> 00:09:33.420
creates that condition, they're not angry
with the judicial system which creates that

00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:35.149
condition, they're angry at the film.

00:09:35.149 --> 00:09:36.149
You see?

00:09:36.149 --> 00:09:38.710
Michael: It’s easier to be angry at the
film?

00:09:38.710 --> 00:09:39.710
Leonard: Yes.

00:09:39.710 --> 00:09:42.800
That I find very, very interesting and that
surprised me.

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:44.170
That really caught me by surprise.

00:09:44.170 --> 00:09:48.370
I thought we are doing a story about a particular
lady.

00:09:48.370 --> 00:09:54.540
We are doing a story about a particular situation
and we have established her character and

00:09:54.540 --> 00:09:59.710
we understand the dilemma in which she finds
herself and we understand the reasons for

00:09:59.710 --> 00:10:00.710
it.

00:10:00.710 --> 00:10:02.180
They're all very clear.

00:10:02.180 --> 00:10:09.930
Now, when things go wrong, the audience would
be much happier if Diane Keaton's character

00:10:09.930 --> 00:10:13.279
could function differently than she does but
she can't because that is her character.

00:10:13.279 --> 00:10:16.750
That is her nature.

00:10:16.750 --> 00:10:21.680
The audience would like to see her … In
this case, she loses custody of her child.

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:27.240
The audience would like to see her do or try
to do something about it even though it's

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:30.730
futile to rise to the occasion as a heroine.

00:10:30.730 --> 00:10:31.820
This is not a heroic person.

00:10:31.820 --> 00:10:36.019
This is a lady who doesn't come visually and
a particular lady with a particular background

00:10:36.019 --> 00:10:38.550
from a particular family.

00:10:38.550 --> 00:10:42.899
The message that I'm getting is that the audience
is saying, "Don't put us in that position.

00:10:42.899 --> 00:10:49.779
Don’t put us in a position where we must
experience with this lady this problem and

00:10:49.779 --> 00:10:54.380
not be able to do anything about it, even
try even though futile to do something about

00:10:54.380 --> 00:10:56.180
it."

00:10:56.180 --> 00:11:01.360
That surprises me and if one were to go back
and remake this movie in order to make it

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:04.130
commercially acceptable and successful, I
think you'd have to do that.

00:11:04.130 --> 00:11:07.970
Michael: What you're talking about is people
used to talk years ago about the Hollywood

00:11:07.970 --> 00:11:12.350
ending that you would turn everything around
even tragic material, and I should say this

00:11:12.350 --> 00:11:14.360
is a heartbreaking movie to watch.

00:11:14.360 --> 00:11:17.570
It is … For those who haven't seen it and
everybody who hasn't seen it, go rent it on

00:11:17.570 --> 00:11:18.570
videocassette.

00:11:18.570 --> 00:11:19.570
It really is worth renting.

00:11:19.570 --> 00:11:22.200
It's a terrific film.

00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:27.730
One of the things about it is it takes you
from absolute luminous romantic high.

00:11:27.730 --> 00:11:31.960
There's a snap in the middle of the movie
where it's almost nightmarish, and yet you

00:11:31.960 --> 00:11:36.880
realize that it's mistakes that the heroine
has made, some carelessness that she's done.

00:11:36.880 --> 00:11:39.980
It's not something that comes out of left
field that is based on character which is

00:11:39.980 --> 00:11:42.040
the best kind of drama, of course.

00:11:42.040 --> 00:11:47.270
With all that, do you suspect that if you
would change the novel, and it was based on

00:11:47.270 --> 00:11:51.180
a bestselling novel, and you would give in
the film "the happy ending" that everybody

00:11:51.180 --> 00:11:56.079
wanted where Diane Keaton is reunited with
her little girl that the film would have had

00:11:56.079 --> 00:11:57.180
the commercial success?

00:11:57.180 --> 00:11:58.860
Leonard: I had no question about it.

00:11:58.860 --> 00:12:00.410
No question about it.

00:12:00.410 --> 00:12:03.779
Michael: Yet you're saying that if you had
it to do over, you wouldn't make that change?

00:12:03.779 --> 00:12:04.779
Leonard: No.

00:12:04.779 --> 00:12:07.070
That film wouldn't interest me.

00:12:07.070 --> 00:12:09.700
At this particular point in time, that film
was not interest me.

00:12:09.700 --> 00:12:14.149
I did it because I felt the film was complex.

00:12:14.149 --> 00:12:21.779
I thought it was challenging and unusual and
those are the reasons that I chose to do the

00:12:21.779 --> 00:12:23.829
picture.

00:12:23.829 --> 00:12:27.010
There were actresses who refused to play the
role for the very reason we're talking about,

00:12:27.010 --> 00:12:30.730
because they did not have the opportunity
to rise to the heroic occasion at the end

00:12:30.730 --> 00:12:34.589
and win out.

00:12:34.589 --> 00:12:36.180
I give Diane Keaton a lot of credit.

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:41.240
She's … If you look back at the body of
her work, it is filled with complex characters,

00:12:41.240 --> 00:12:48.050
characters of humanity and ambiguity and frailties
and … Diane is not an actress who goes into

00:12:48.050 --> 00:12:50.149
a film worrying about what the audience are
going to think of her.

00:12:50.149 --> 00:12:55.040
She's only concerned about what is necessary
to serve the character in the film.

00:12:55.040 --> 00:12:58.339
If you think less of her at the end of the
movie because she's … because you associated

00:12:58.339 --> 00:13:01.100
her with this character, so be it but that
to me is artistry.

00:13:01.100 --> 00:13:03.290
Michael: What a great performance.

00:13:03.290 --> 00:13:06.019
The question … and this raises a larger
question and you'll pardon me for getting

00:13:06.019 --> 00:13:12.959
into this right away … which is who … to
whom is your highest obligation as a filmmaker?

00:13:12.959 --> 00:13:19.209
Do you visualize it … Is it to your collaborators,
is it to the novelist whose work you're adapting

00:13:19.209 --> 00:13:22.170
or is it to the audience that you're attempting
to reach?

00:13:22.170 --> 00:13:25.950
Leonard: I don't think … I think you've
left out a key ingredient and that is the

00:13:25.950 --> 00:13:28.190
studio that pays for it.

00:13:28.190 --> 00:13:31.610
Michael: That's what's a few million dollars
between friends, right?

00:13:31.610 --> 00:13:32.660
Leonard: It is.

00:13:32.660 --> 00:13:34.310
It is actually their movie.

00:13:34.310 --> 00:13:35.310
It's their film.

00:13:35.310 --> 00:13:38.170
They own it and they commissioned you to make
it.

00:13:38.170 --> 00:13:41.450
Before you set out to make it, you're going
to have some pretty intense conversation with

00:13:41.450 --> 00:13:45.201
them about what this film is going to be about,
how you see the film, how … what is your

00:13:45.201 --> 00:13:46.329
perception of this movie?

00:13:46.329 --> 00:13:52.340
In this particular case as you can imagine,
it being a Touchstone movie and I think their

00:13:52.340 --> 00:13:58.070
first that was not of the Three Men and a
Baby commercial type, they were particularly

00:13:58.070 --> 00:14:02.470
interested and concerned in what I had to
say about how … what … how I saw the film.

00:14:02.470 --> 00:14:08.620
It is to their credit that they never once
said, "Let's soften this ending.

00:14:08.620 --> 00:14:13.120
Let's not go out on this painful note.

00:14:13.120 --> 00:14:16.720
We're essentially doing a tragic story here."

00:14:16.720 --> 00:14:25.800
They never once asked me to avoid that or
change that or mitigating that in any way.

00:14:25.800 --> 00:14:30.220
In that sense, at least in the sense that
you have a conversation with them before you

00:14:30.220 --> 00:14:36.070
start to make the picture, then you owe it
to them to try to hue to that concept.

00:14:36.070 --> 00:14:39.089
Then after that, the process takes over.

00:14:39.089 --> 00:14:49.690
You're in the process now and your responsibility
is to your actors, the script, the writers,

00:14:49.690 --> 00:14:55.579
and particularly to yourself to try to see
through the … you … for me, to give the

00:14:55.579 --> 00:14:59.740
film something personal so that it is not
simply a film that anyone else could have

00:14:59.740 --> 00:15:00.740
done.

00:15:00.740 --> 00:15:04.300
It … I'm trying to read through some kind
of personal touch into these films, to remake

00:15:04.300 --> 00:15:07.860
some kind of a personal investment, and the
audience is always in mind.

00:15:07.860 --> 00:15:10.019
If I'm doing a comedy, I want to hear laughter.

00:15:10.019 --> 00:15:11.700
I want that audience laughing at it.

00:15:11.700 --> 00:15:16.500
If they're not laughing, then I failed myself,
I failed the audience, I failed the whole

00:15:16.500 --> 00:15:17.500
project.

00:15:17.500 --> 00:15:20.790
In the Three … In The Good Mother, if they're
not touched, I have failed.

00:15:20.790 --> 00:15:24.850
Now it's interesting that you asked about
the novelist.

00:15:24.850 --> 00:15:29.690
I had lengthy conversations with her, Sue
Miller, who wrote the novels, who's a bestselling

00:15:29.690 --> 00:15:33.860
novel, and essentially the film follows the
path of the novel.

00:15:33.860 --> 00:15:38.340
The novel was much more dense and detailed
which we … you don’t have time to serve

00:15:38.340 --> 00:15:39.990
in the film.

00:15:39.990 --> 00:15:42.981
I think essentially the film is truly not
what … I don't think she's seen the film

00:15:42.981 --> 00:15:46.440
yet and I can understand why.

00:15:46.440 --> 00:15:52.220
I had lengthy conversations with her during
the preparation and one final conversation

00:15:52.220 --> 00:15:57.220
the day we started filming in Cambridge, Massachusetts
where she lives and she came on the set to

00:15:57.220 --> 00:16:05.020
visit, and on that day, I said to her, "This
must be a strange experience to you.

00:16:05.020 --> 00:16:09.670
Novelist, successful first time, first novel,
now being made into a film and you really

00:16:09.670 --> 00:16:11.029
have nothing to do with it in a way."

00:16:11.029 --> 00:16:14.940
She didn’t write the script and she's not
participating into making the film, "It must

00:16:14.940 --> 00:16:18.550
seem that it is sailing away from you."

00:16:18.550 --> 00:16:23.640
She said that she had come to terms with it
and she told me an interesting story.

00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:35.930
She said that a good friend of hers had written
Ironweed and a screenplay and she said he

00:16:35.930 --> 00:16:40.779
invited her and her husband to see the film
at a previous screening and they were very

00:16:40.779 --> 00:16:42.940
disappointed in the film.

00:16:42.940 --> 00:16:45.750
The novelist-screenwriter thought the film
was wonderful.

00:16:45.750 --> 00:16:50.470
Of course, he was a novelist and a screenwriter
and he was very subjectively involved.

00:16:50.470 --> 00:16:53.790
She said what she discovered was that he doesn't
know anything about making a movie.

00:16:53.790 --> 00:16:58.621
He knew how to write the novel but he's not
a filmmaker and the film had no tension, no

00:16:58.621 --> 00:17:00.570
design, no form, no shape.

00:17:00.570 --> 00:17:01.570
It was formless.

00:17:01.570 --> 00:17:07.300
It was … Anyway, she said, "You people know
how to make movies.

00:17:07.300 --> 00:17:08.870
I don't know anything about it.

00:17:08.870 --> 00:17:11.919
You go and make the movie," and when the movie
was finished and I invited her to come to

00:17:11.919 --> 00:17:15.539
see it, she said, "I won't be able to see
it for a long time to come."

00:17:15.539 --> 00:17:19.350
She said, "I certainly won't be able to able
to see it with you in the room," because she

00:17:19.350 --> 00:17:22.140
said, "The last thing in the world I want
to do is when the lights come up, have to

00:17:22.140 --> 00:17:26.830
face you and tell you what I thought of it."

00:17:26.830 --> 00:17:31.760
She separated herself which was very comfortable
for me but, yes, she would just … finally

00:17:31.760 --> 00:17:34.460
to answer your question, yes, I was trying
to satisfy her as well.

00:17:34.460 --> 00:17:39.080
Michael: Speaking of you being in the room
when she saw the film, that's one thing you

00:17:39.080 --> 00:17:40.150
always wondered about.

00:17:40.150 --> 00:17:44.110
You've had the experience of this phenomenally
successful film, "Three Men and a Baby" which

00:17:44.110 --> 00:17:48.090
I think is your first film directed in which
you didn't appear as an actor, first feature

00:17:48.090 --> 00:17:50.400
film as what …
Leonard: That's right.

00:17:50.400 --> 00:17:56.030
Michael: With that film, was there one moment
or one screening when you saw it for the first

00:17:56.030 --> 00:18:00.880
time with an audience and you realized, "Wait
a minute, this is going to hit," what was

00:18:00.880 --> 00:18:01.880
that like?

00:18:01.880 --> 00:18:04.520
What was …
Leonard: Yes.

00:18:04.520 --> 00:18:15.590
I have a house in Lake Tahoe in Nevada and
the picture had shot well.

00:18:15.590 --> 00:18:21.830
We had finished a little bit ahead of schedule
and we had saved some money and I asked the

00:18:21.830 --> 00:18:26.270
studio … Normally, you would negotiate for
this in advance but I said, "I would like

00:18:26.270 --> 00:18:31.100
very much to take the editors with me and
go to Incline Village, Nevada for a month

00:18:31.100 --> 00:18:32.120
and edit there.

00:18:32.120 --> 00:18:36.640
We'll rent some space and create some editing
facilities and if you would truck some stuff.

00:18:36.640 --> 00:18:37.690
I have a house up there.

00:18:37.690 --> 00:18:39.920
I think it would be helpful to all of us."

00:18:39.920 --> 00:18:43.860
It costs them some money and you would say
they didn't need … they didn't owe me, but

00:18:43.860 --> 00:18:46.000
they said, "Yes, go and do that."

00:18:46.000 --> 00:18:52.460
We spent a month at the end of which time
we had a cut and we put together some temporary

00:18:52.460 --> 00:18:59.230
music tracks and so forth and we brought in
a projector, a projection system.

00:18:59.230 --> 00:19:01.130
For the dual system, it's complicated.

00:19:01.130 --> 00:19:04.891
You have … at that point, you don't have
the film composited with the soundtracks so

00:19:04.891 --> 00:19:09.490
you can't just put at your local movie houses,
take around the machine.

00:19:09.490 --> 00:19:10.490
We brought in the necessary equipment.

00:19:10.490 --> 00:19:14.110
We announced too in the village, the Incline
Village, there would be a sneak preview of

00:19:14.110 --> 00:19:22.840
a film this coming Friday night or whatever,
and the place was full, 400 or 500 people.

00:19:22.840 --> 00:19:27.289
There were lengthy periods of time where you
could not hear the dialogue because there

00:19:27.289 --> 00:19:33.870
was so much laughter and people recognized
me afterward and were saying things like,

00:19:33.870 --> 00:19:35.590
"Thanks … This was key.

00:19:35.590 --> 00:19:39.230
Thanks for making me feel so good."

00:19:39.230 --> 00:19:43.549
I knew that we had a potential monster on
our hands.

00:19:43.549 --> 00:19:45.429
People would want to come out of a movie feeling
good.

00:19:45.429 --> 00:19:46.960
Michael: Yes, they do.

00:19:46.960 --> 00:19:49.010
Leonard: That's part of the process.

00:19:49.010 --> 00:19:52.200
Michael: Speaking about that, this afternoon,
one of the things we're talking about, we're

00:19:52.200 --> 00:19:55.919
talking about Three Men and a Baby in the
context of a series of movies that seemed

00:19:55.919 --> 00:20:03.120
to come out in 1987 that sort of returned
America to its fascination and love affair

00:20:03.120 --> 00:20:06.970
with children which hadn't been seen in movies
for a long time.

00:20:06.970 --> 00:20:09.970
Were you conscious when you were making a
film of being part of that?

00:20:09.970 --> 00:20:11.240
Was that on your mind?

00:20:11.240 --> 00:20:12.240
Leonard: Yes or no.

00:20:12.240 --> 00:20:16.070
I was … The irony of it is that I was most
concerned and conscious of one other film

00:20:16.070 --> 00:20:17.909
which was starring Diane Keaton.

00:20:17.909 --> 00:20:19.710
Michael: Baby Boom?

00:20:19.710 --> 00:20:20.909
Leonard: Yes.

00:20:20.909 --> 00:20:24.500
When I saw it, I was very nervous for the
first 10 to 15 minutes.

00:20:24.500 --> 00:20:26.440
I thought they're doing the same jokes.

00:20:26.440 --> 00:20:29.880
She's having troubles diapering this baby
and so are her guys.

00:20:29.880 --> 00:20:32.840
Michael: It's funnier when guys do it.

00:20:32.840 --> 00:20:34.240
Leonard: Yes.

00:20:34.240 --> 00:20:36.669
That's true.

00:20:36.669 --> 00:20:43.040
Essentially, except for that very, very important
difference, here was a lady who was unprepared

00:20:43.040 --> 00:20:49.630
… a person who was unprepared for in having
to deal with an infant and so were our guys.

00:20:49.630 --> 00:20:51.539
I worried for the first 10 to 15 minutes.

00:20:51.539 --> 00:20:58.771
After that, Baby Boom really went off in a
whole other direction and I realized that

00:20:58.771 --> 00:21:01.659
we had two uncanny different films and they
were both successful.

00:21:01.659 --> 00:21:02.659
Baby Boom was successful.

00:21:02.659 --> 00:21:03.659
Michael: Yes, it was.

00:21:03.659 --> 00:21:08.630
Leonard: In fact, it was the success of Baby
Boom for Diane that was very helpful in getting

00:21:08.630 --> 00:21:13.280
me to … helping me to convince Disney that
I should use her in The Good Mother.

00:21:13.280 --> 00:21:18.370
Michael: Were you … I guess going back to
this question, we've been talking a lot in

00:21:18.370 --> 00:21:20.340
the last 2 days when I'm in touch being with
people here …

00:21:20.340 --> 00:21:21.750
Leonard: About the baby?

00:21:21.750 --> 00:21:23.809
Michael: … no, about values, about films
and values.

00:21:23.809 --> 00:21:24.809
Leonard: Yes.

00:21:24.809 --> 00:21:28.659
Michael: There's no question that compared
to a lot of the comedies that you've had earlier

00:21:28.659 --> 00:21:34.570
in the 80s and a lot of the films in general,
that the values that one can take away from

00:21:34.570 --> 00:21:42.440
Three Men and a Baby were wholesome, reassuring,
more traditional in terms of … was that

00:21:42.440 --> 00:21:45.770
a conscious aspect of your filmmaking at all?

00:21:45.770 --> 00:21:46.770
Leonard: Yes.

00:21:46.770 --> 00:21:52.470
It's interesting that you say more traditional
because at the end of the film, you have a

00:21:52.470 --> 00:21:54.650
very non-traditional family structure.

00:21:54.650 --> 00:21:55.650
Michael: Yes, indeed.

00:21:55.650 --> 00:21:59.570
Leonard: You have three fathers and a mother
and there's no marriage involved.

00:21:59.570 --> 00:22:01.430
Michael: Right, the mother is not involved
with any of them apparently.

00:22:01.430 --> 00:22:02.430
Leonard: That's right.

00:22:02.430 --> 00:22:07.520
You have an entirely new kind of family unit.

00:22:07.520 --> 00:22:10.710
She says … When she says at the end they've
invited her to move in, she says, "Do you

00:22:10.710 --> 00:22:13.200
have room?" and Selleck says, "I'll build
you a room."

00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:15.260
It gets a big laugh.

00:22:15.260 --> 00:22:17.780
Everybody loves that.

00:22:17.780 --> 00:22:22.570
Very fresh, I thought, the ending in that
sense.

00:22:22.570 --> 00:22:29.679
On the other hand, what I was shooting for,
what I was trying for in the development of

00:22:29.679 --> 00:22:35.730
the script and the development of the characters
was to deal with a Peter Pan Syndrome with

00:22:35.730 --> 00:22:43.660
these three guys who just didn't want to grow
up and that's not particularly new.

00:22:43.660 --> 00:22:44.660
Michael: No.

00:22:44.660 --> 00:22:47.730
Leonard: I think they did it very well.

00:22:47.730 --> 00:22:52.940
The very concept … the concept of … the
decisions of what they did, the choice of

00:22:52.940 --> 00:22:58.250
their careers, for example, Selleck was an
architect who had always been building things

00:22:58.250 --> 00:23:05.600
as a kid and was building bigger things as
an adult; Guttenberg was in cartoon line,

00:23:05.600 --> 00:23:13.679
literally playing with childish figures and
childish ideas; and Ted Danson was functioning

00:23:13.679 --> 00:23:17.000
in a profession which we literally call playing.

00:23:17.000 --> 00:23:18.750
He was playing roles.

00:23:18.750 --> 00:23:25.340
They were all children in that respect and
the structure was to put them in a position

00:23:25.340 --> 00:23:31.580
where they had to begin to feel some sense
of responsibility to something other … someone

00:23:31.580 --> 00:23:32.850
other than themselves.

00:23:32.850 --> 00:23:34.880
I think we did well.

00:23:34.880 --> 00:23:35.880
Michael: Yes.

00:23:35.880 --> 00:23:36.880
Okay, baby.

00:23:36.880 --> 00:23:37.880
It's time to grow up.

00:23:37.880 --> 00:23:38.880
Leonard: Yes, exactly.

00:23:38.880 --> 00:23:40.640
Michael: Actually, we were talking about this
also before.

00:23:40.640 --> 00:23:45.140
I know that you inherited this project, you
had mentioned before.

00:23:45.140 --> 00:23:48.640
To what extent did you change, alter, revise
the script?

00:23:48.640 --> 00:23:49.830
Leonard: A lot, a lot.

00:23:49.830 --> 00:23:53.320
The script was very French when I came on
the job.

00:23:53.320 --> 00:23:56.480
The script had been developed under a French
director.

00:23:56.480 --> 00:24:01.980
The lady, Coline Serreau, who had written
and directed the French version was on the

00:24:01.980 --> 00:24:07.160
job for Disney to … not to write but to
direct the American version.

00:24:07.160 --> 00:24:11.659
She had writers who were writing under her
supervision who were doing as what essentially

00:24:11.659 --> 00:24:14.490
was a translation of the French film.

00:24:14.490 --> 00:24:15.750
It was very far in nature.

00:24:15.750 --> 00:24:17.710
The tone of it was foreign.

00:24:17.710 --> 00:24:22.840
The nature of the characters, I felt, was
inaccessible.

00:24:22.840 --> 00:24:26.340
It might have been an enormous success but
she left the project, I came on and I had

00:24:26.340 --> 00:24:29.409
to do something that I could get in touch
with, so we changed very word of the script,

00:24:29.409 --> 00:24:32.679
just reconstructed the whole thing in a 5-week
period.

00:24:32.679 --> 00:24:34.130
Michael: In 5 weeks?

00:24:34.130 --> 00:24:35.130
Leonard: Yeah.

00:24:35.130 --> 00:24:36.130
Michael: Good luck.

00:24:36.130 --> 00:24:37.130
Leonard: Yeah.

00:24:37.130 --> 00:24:38.130
Michael: It worked out.

00:24:38.130 --> 00:24:40.760
The … Two more questions about Three Men
and a Baby.

00:24:40.760 --> 00:24:44.590
You'll pardon me, the one question that I
had about … The one thing about the film

00:24:44.590 --> 00:24:48.250
that troubled me a little bit and it's something
else that I spoke about earlier here, so I

00:24:48.250 --> 00:24:54.210
think in order to be honest, I should bring
it up with you was the subplot about the drug

00:24:54.210 --> 00:24:55.210
deal.

00:24:55.210 --> 00:24:56.400
Leonard: It would never go away.

00:24:56.400 --> 00:24:57.900
I just couldn't get it to go away.

00:24:57.900 --> 00:24:59.990
Michael: You wanted it to go away?

00:24:59.990 --> 00:25:07.360
Leonard: We tried desperately to find any
other scheme.

00:25:07.360 --> 00:25:14.299
One of the most agonizing problems that I
had to deal with was to find a way to play

00:25:14.299 --> 00:25:19.270
a scene when 2 supposedly intelligent guys
like Tom Selleck and Steve Guttenberg hand

00:25:19.270 --> 00:25:25.340
over a baby, an infant, to 2 characters who
are drug dealers, who come and say, "Where's

00:25:25.340 --> 00:25:26.340
the package?

00:25:26.340 --> 00:25:27.340
Give us the package.

00:25:27.340 --> 00:25:28.650
We're here for the package."

00:25:28.650 --> 00:25:29.650
Yes.

00:25:29.650 --> 00:25:32.740
very difficult to pull off, I thought.

00:25:32.740 --> 00:25:33.750
There was no complaint about it.

00:25:33.750 --> 00:25:35.820
I wouldn't have believed if I said … I'd
say, "Oh, no.

00:25:35.820 --> 00:25:41.640
We're not going to give the baby with these
2 obviously hoodlums."

00:25:41.640 --> 00:25:43.230
We got away with it.

00:25:43.230 --> 00:25:48.049
The audience's suspense of disbelief was enormous,
enormous.

00:25:48.049 --> 00:25:53.309
Then we re-wrote for weeks during the shooting
the picture, the whole exchange where they

00:25:53.309 --> 00:25:57.350
go finally to get rid of these guys who get
them caught by the police.

00:25:57.350 --> 00:26:00.900
We had written in … under all kinds of circumstances
including a basketball game with the 5 of

00:26:00.900 --> 00:26:03.720
them played and the drugs are in the basketball.

00:26:03.720 --> 00:26:07.820
It was … You wouldn't believe the permutations
that this went through.

00:26:07.820 --> 00:26:14.511
It was … I just … I wanted to do it as
quickly and as offhandedly as possible and

00:26:14.511 --> 00:26:15.511
be done with it.

00:26:15.511 --> 00:26:19.340
Michael: He … I guess the question and I
don't want to beat this to death, but here

00:26:19.340 --> 00:26:24.590
is a … as close as Hollywood comes ever
to a real … to a wholesome family comedy.

00:26:24.590 --> 00:26:29.330
It's really something you can take your Aunt
Tilly to see and your grandmother and little

00:26:29.330 --> 00:26:30.940
kids and everybody.

00:26:30.940 --> 00:26:35.580
Here all of a sudden in the midst of this
film is this subplot about criminals and drug

00:26:35.580 --> 00:26:38.200
dealing and it gets pretty tense at certain
moments.

00:26:38.200 --> 00:26:41.750
Did it ever occur to you or other people involved
in the picture, "Wait a minute.

00:26:41.750 --> 00:26:42.950
We don't need that.

00:26:42.950 --> 00:26:47.080
Why are we throwing …?"
Leonard: It's not that we didn't need … we

00:26:47.080 --> 00:26:48.679
needed some kind of device.

00:26:48.679 --> 00:26:56.150
We needed a device where they mistakenly give
the baby away and that's what we represented

00:26:56.150 --> 00:26:57.150
with it.

00:26:57.150 --> 00:27:03.860
Under the circumstances with all of the other
facets of the film having to be changed, we

00:27:03.860 --> 00:27:05.720
just … we ended up having to live with it.

00:27:05.720 --> 00:27:07.100
It was the best we could do.

00:27:07.100 --> 00:27:08.310
We should have done better.

00:27:08.310 --> 00:27:09.310
We simply couldn't.

00:27:09.310 --> 00:27:11.290
Michael: It worked out okay? (laughs)
Leonard: Yeah.

00:27:11.290 --> 00:27:13.920
Michael: The one last question about it, the
famous scene …

00:27:13.920 --> 00:27:16.221
Leonard: It's interesting that you say that
this is a wholesome film for which you can

00:27:16.221 --> 00:27:17.409
bring your Aunt Tilly.

00:27:17.409 --> 00:27:20.919
Stop and think about what that says about
what's acceptable today in a movie.

00:27:20.919 --> 00:27:26.500
Here is a child born out of wedlock, and the
father doesn't even know the child exists.

00:27:26.500 --> 00:27:28.549
The father comes home and says, "That's not
my baby."

00:27:28.549 --> 00:27:32.240
Here comes the mother … and the mother has
dropped the baby off on the doorstep without

00:27:32.240 --> 00:27:36.559
a face-to-face confrontation, you see, just
drop that baby off and walk away.

00:27:36.559 --> 00:27:41.669
All things are kind of shocking if you look
at them in terms of what's acceptable behavior.

00:27:41.669 --> 00:27:42.669
Michael: That's today.

00:27:42.669 --> 00:27:49.010
Do you know that today statistically 20% of
all American babies … all American babies

00:27:49.010 --> 00:27:50.440
are born out of wedlock?

00:27:50.440 --> 00:27:55.150
Leonard: I'm not surprised with that statistic
because during my research for The Good Mother,

00:27:55.150 --> 00:27:59.831
I discovered that something like 50% of the
children in the United States today are in

00:27:59.831 --> 00:28:02.840
single-parent homes and growing.

00:28:02.840 --> 00:28:06.799
Michael: Last question about Three Men and
a Baby to get us on a happier note, the famous

00:28:06.799 --> 00:28:10.970
scene where the three guys are singing "Goodnight
Sweetheart," was that scripted?

00:28:10.970 --> 00:28:11.970
Was that your idea?

00:28:11.970 --> 00:28:12.970
Leonard: Yes.

00:28:12.970 --> 00:28:13.970
No, it was script- yes.

00:28:13.970 --> 00:28:14.970
No, it wasn't my idea.

00:28:14.970 --> 00:28:18.260
No, it was … I've forgotten whether it was
in the original version or not but it was

00:28:18.260 --> 00:28:21.420
in the script that I inherited.

00:28:21.420 --> 00:28:22.420
Yes.

00:28:22.420 --> 00:28:24.830
I thought, "How corny."

00:28:24.830 --> 00:28:27.570
Leonard: We did it.

00:28:27.570 --> 00:28:28.570
No shame.

00:28:28.570 --> 00:28:29.669
Michael: Listen, we're talking about movies.

00:28:29.669 --> 00:28:38.770
It's not … Speaking of no shame, so just
to cover so that we are doing justice to everything,

00:28:38.770 --> 00:28:41.370
to cover Star Trek for a moment.

00:28:41.370 --> 00:28:49.059
A vastly entertaining film, another very successful
film with a message and the message being,

00:28:49.059 --> 00:28:51.060
to simplify it, "Save the whales."

00:28:51.060 --> 00:28:53.220
Leonard: Save the world.

00:28:53.220 --> 00:28:54.300
Michael: True.

00:28:54.300 --> 00:28:55.300
True.

00:28:55.300 --> 00:29:00.580
To what extent was that message important
to you as a filmmaker while you were doing

00:29:00.580 --> 00:29:01.580
the picture?

00:29:01.580 --> 00:29:02.580
Leonard: That was very important.

00:29:02.580 --> 00:29:04.950
It was very important, but I wanted to … I
wanted it to be a fun adventure.

00:29:04.950 --> 00:29:13.240
I didn't want to be hammering people over
the head with a problem.

00:29:13.240 --> 00:29:20.200
We had done 2 pictures, Star Trek's 1 and
2 … or 3 pictures, Star Trek's 1, 2, and

00:29:20.200 --> 00:29:24.919
3, all of which were pretty intense.

00:29:24.919 --> 00:29:27.760
There was a lot of dying.

00:29:27.760 --> 00:29:31.130
There was death and resurrection, if you will.

00:29:31.130 --> 00:29:33.500
Michael: They didn't get rid of Mr. Spock?

00:29:33.500 --> 00:29:35.500
Leonard: No.

00:29:35.500 --> 00:29:44.080
It was intense stuff and I thought it was
really time to recapture some of the fun that

00:29:44.080 --> 00:29:48.950
we'd had making the series, which we'd had
brought a little of in the 3 movies.

00:29:48.950 --> 00:29:56.499
I also wanted very much to do a film in which
there was no particular nasty villain that

00:29:56.499 --> 00:30:03.790
… again, circumstances, lack of awareness,
unconsciousness, whatever would create the

00:30:03.790 --> 00:30:06.490
problem.

00:30:06.490 --> 00:30:11.419
Given that we were going to do a time travel
story and given also that we're coming back

00:30:11.419 --> 00:30:17.899
to earth now to find a solution to a problem
that's taking place in the 21st Century, the

00:30:17.899 --> 00:30:22.240
question was what to come back for, what is
it that's missing in the 21st Century that

00:30:22.240 --> 00:30:23.340
we need.

00:30:23.340 --> 00:30:28.980
I'd read a book called Biophilia written by
a Harvard biologist in which he talked about

00:30:28.980 --> 00:30:34.900
the fact that by 1990, we will be losing something
like 10,000 species per year off this planet,

00:30:34.900 --> 00:30:40.260
many of them never having even been recorded,
discovered or recorded.

00:30:40.260 --> 00:30:45.450
Then when you start to lose species in that
large a number, you have to be consumed with

00:30:45.450 --> 00:30:49.442
what they call the "Keystone Species Theory"
which is that … It's like a house of cards.

00:30:49.442 --> 00:30:53.020
You can take a card away here, you can take
a card away there and the house stands but

00:30:53.020 --> 00:30:56.520
eventually if you continue to take cards away,
you will finally get to a card that was a

00:30:56.520 --> 00:31:01.230
keystone card and that card will cause … the
loss of that card will cause others to collapse

00:31:01.230 --> 00:31:05.240
and others to collapse and so forth down the
chain.

00:31:05.240 --> 00:31:14.440
The idea was that we would … we have hit
a keystone and we now have … and the problem

00:31:14.440 --> 00:31:18.190
is developing in the 21st Century, we have
to come back to the 20th Century to find that

00:31:18.190 --> 00:31:20.960
thing and solve the problem.

00:31:20.960 --> 00:31:25.409
You're going to come back for a mosquito.

00:31:25.409 --> 00:31:28.850
You're going to come back for a plant.

00:31:28.850 --> 00:31:34.180
None of those seemed particularly interesting
visually cinematically.

00:31:34.180 --> 00:31:39.340
When I struck on the idea of the whales, not
only the size and the grandeur of them, but

00:31:39.340 --> 00:31:45.370
also the mysterious song that they sing that
has never really been quite explained.

00:31:45.370 --> 00:31:48.250
We don't know exactly why they do it.

00:31:48.250 --> 00:31:53.980
That led me to another interesting subject
which always intrigues which is communication

00:31:53.980 --> 00:31:57.899
or failure in communication.

00:31:57.899 --> 00:32:00.299
I was just taken with the whole thing.

00:32:00.299 --> 00:32:02.330
I started doing a lot of research.

00:32:02.330 --> 00:32:05.180
I'd looked at a lot of films with whales and
I'd contacted all the whale institution … but

00:32:05.180 --> 00:32:08.990
you see, my consciousness had already been
raised by the Greenpeace people and I give

00:32:08.990 --> 00:32:10.360
them the credit.

00:32:10.360 --> 00:32:15.470
They're the ones who first put me in such
the idea that people must do something about

00:32:15.470 --> 00:32:16.470
this.

00:32:16.470 --> 00:32:19.360
I was … I thought that it was great drama
years ago when they got up in their rubber

00:32:19.360 --> 00:32:24.960
boats and planted themselves out in the ocean
between the whales and the wedding ships and

00:32:24.960 --> 00:32:26.490
were in danger being harpooned.

00:32:26.490 --> 00:32:29.809
Boy, that's dramatic stuff.

00:32:29.809 --> 00:32:32.130
They made me conscious of this concern.

00:32:32.130 --> 00:32:36.600
Michael: Have you had any indication from
either the Greenpeace people or anyone else

00:32:36.600 --> 00:32:39.100
about the film's impact and …
Leonard: Yeah.

00:32:39.100 --> 00:32:41.440
Boy, I hear from them all the time, all the
time.

00:32:41.440 --> 00:32:43.299
Michael: What do they tell you?

00:32:43.299 --> 00:32:48.309
Leonard: That there's a big awareness that
I have … auditioning to raise consciousness.

00:32:48.309 --> 00:32:55.140
I was invited to Russia when the film was
finished, let's see, about a year and a half

00:32:55.140 --> 00:32:56.140
ago.

00:32:56.140 --> 00:32:59.270
No, after the film was released ago, about
a year and a half ago, I was invited to go

00:32:59.270 --> 00:33:07.789
to Russia to show the film in Moscow to help
celebrate the event when the Russian government

00:33:07.789 --> 00:33:11.210
declared a ban on all commercial whaling.

00:33:11.210 --> 00:33:12.390
There was an awareness even there.

00:33:12.390 --> 00:33:15.750
Michael: The Soviets were quite late in declaring
that ban too, I think, yes.

00:33:15.750 --> 00:33:16.750
Leonard: Yes.

00:33:16.750 --> 00:33:17.750
That's right.

00:33:17.750 --> 00:33:19.330
Michael: You haven't been invited to Japan
which is still doing commercial whaling, right?

00:33:19.330 --> 00:33:20.330
Leonard: Yes.

00:33:20.330 --> 00:33:21.330
No, that's right.

00:33:21.330 --> 00:33:25.529
Michael: That's the next step.

00:33:25.529 --> 00:33:32.350
In terms of your work as a filmmaker and as
an actor, are there certain kinds of issues,

00:33:32.350 --> 00:33:39.250
certain kinds of aspects of human life that
you think movies, Hollywood does not handle

00:33:39.250 --> 00:33:45.049
well that simply don't lend themselves to
effective treatment on screen?

00:33:45.049 --> 00:33:48.149
Leonard: I don’t know if I can answer your
question directly.

00:33:48.149 --> 00:33:56.679
It does seem to me that because of the nature
of what the film is and what the concerns

00:33:56.679 --> 00:33:59.290
are in making the film, particularly those
concerns that I talked about with The Good

00:33:59.290 --> 00:34:07.760
Mother and the audience, that we too often
find it useful to distort subject matter.

00:34:07.760 --> 00:34:10.220
I think Mississippi Burning is a big distortion.

00:34:10.220 --> 00:34:13.510
The picture was offered to me and I couldn’t
do it.

00:34:13.510 --> 00:34:17.960
Michael: The picture with the Chris Gerolmo's
… with the script, the drama script?

00:34:17.960 --> 00:34:18.960
Leonard: The job was offered.

00:34:18.960 --> 00:34:19.960
The Chris Gerolmo's script.

00:34:19.960 --> 00:34:20.960
Yeah.

00:34:20.960 --> 00:34:21.960
Michael: Wow.

00:34:21.960 --> 00:34:22.960
I didn't know.

00:34:22.960 --> 00:34:23.960
Leonard: The job was offered to me and I couldn't
do it.

00:34:23.960 --> 00:34:24.960
Michael: Why couldn't you do it?

00:34:24.960 --> 00:34:32.030
Leonard: I really didn't think that my presentation
of the film, my vision of the film was what

00:34:32.030 --> 00:34:33.850
the company and what the audience want.

00:34:33.850 --> 00:34:35.160
Michael: How would it have been different?

00:34:35.160 --> 00:34:36.991
Leonard: I just couldn’t do it.

00:34:36.991 --> 00:34:39.349
It would have been an entirely different movie,
entirely different movie.

00:34:39.349 --> 00:34:43.780
Alan Parker who made the film has said that
the only way the film could be made is the

00:34:43.780 --> 00:34:49.619
way he did it which is to make the film about
white people doing the job and that admission

00:34:49.619 --> 00:34:52.859
in itself tells me there's a major distortion
because that's not what the Civil Rights Movement

00:34:52.859 --> 00:34:55.030
was about.

00:34:55.030 --> 00:35:02.800
Now, given that statement, there is this ongoing
discussion about should one make the movie

00:35:02.800 --> 00:35:08.430
then and, of course, a lot of people say yes
because the film finally does say that repression

00:35:08.430 --> 00:35:11.070
is wrong, and that's a good thing.

00:35:11.070 --> 00:35:12.500
That's a good statement to make.

00:35:12.500 --> 00:35:17.710
I take issue with a television miniseries
that was done called "Holocaust" a few years

00:35:17.710 --> 00:35:18.710
ago.

00:35:18.710 --> 00:35:19.710
I did not like it at all.

00:35:19.710 --> 00:35:20.710
I thought it trivialized.

00:35:20.710 --> 00:35:32.460
I thought it reduced to a pathetic level this
gigantic event.

00:35:32.460 --> 00:35:36.869
There are others who said and I'm sure that
there's a truth in it, that getting it on

00:35:36.869 --> 00:35:41.609
the air was important because there were millions
and millions of people who would not even

00:35:41.609 --> 00:35:45.630
have been conscious of what the event … that
the event took place or what it was all about

00:35:45.630 --> 00:35:53.060
or what was involved if they hadn't seen that
miniseries and I'm sure that's true.

00:35:53.060 --> 00:35:56.740
You have to finally do these things on the
most personal level.

00:35:56.740 --> 00:36:01.271
The question is how … will I feel comfortable
spending the next 7 or 8 months of my life

00:36:01.271 --> 00:36:05.540
making that film and the answer was no, I
don't think so because I would be unhappy

00:36:05.540 --> 00:36:08.450
about some of things that I would have to
do to make the film work.

00:36:08.450 --> 00:36:12.970
Michael: Would I be … would it be accurate
to describe your position or your decision

00:36:12.970 --> 00:36:15.790
to turn down that film which I had no idea
about?

00:36:15.790 --> 00:36:17.109
Was that a political decision?

00:36:17.109 --> 00:36:20.400
Leonard: I would say it was of social decision.

00:36:20.400 --> 00:36:22.490
I suppose political but more social.

00:36:22.490 --> 00:36:33.790
I'm more concerned about doing justice to
what the event … what eventually took place.

00:36:33.790 --> 00:36:38.780
I think we all know that the FBI was not that
literally involved, that Diego Hugo was not

00:36:38.780 --> 00:36:42.800
that happy about having FBI and men go down
there all, that his hand had to be forced

00:36:42.800 --> 00:36:47.800
to send the first FBI man down, and now the
picture, they make it seem so simple.

00:36:47.800 --> 00:36:50.410
When they run through a problem, they go with
a phone booth, they need 100 more men and

00:36:50.410 --> 00:36:52.300
the next day there are 100 more FBI men down.

00:36:52.300 --> 00:36:54.060
That simply was not the case.

00:36:54.060 --> 00:36:57.510
There were no blacks in the FBI as we know
except for Hugo's …

00:36:57.510 --> 00:36:58.780
Michael: Driver?

00:36:58.780 --> 00:37:01.329
Leonard: … driver, his chauffeur.

00:37:01.329 --> 00:37:04.290
When they do bring down a black agent, he's
a terrorist.

00:37:04.290 --> 00:37:08.250
He comes out with a razorblade and he's going
to do terrible things to the mayor of the

00:37:08.250 --> 00:37:10.710
town if the mayor doesn't give him the information
he needs.

00:37:10.710 --> 00:37:13.660
He becomes a vigilante.

00:37:13.660 --> 00:37:17.520
I just think it's a distortion of what's right.

00:37:17.520 --> 00:37:20.301
Michael: Just a couple more questions and
then we'll turn it over with…

00:37:20.301 --> 00:37:23.150
Leonard: The picture was well done, and I
mean that.

00:37:23.150 --> 00:37:30.040
It's very slick, good filmmaking and it pleases
audiences because right wins out at the end

00:37:30.040 --> 00:37:31.040
of this.

00:37:31.040 --> 00:37:35.820
Michael: We have just a couple more questions
and we'll turn this over to the panel.

00:37:35.820 --> 00:37:43.350
Is there a dream project that Leonard Nimoy
as a filmmaker would like to do and has dreamed

00:37:43.350 --> 00:37:45.460
of doing but hasn't gotten together yet?

00:37:45.460 --> 00:37:49.660
Leonard: I wish there was because I'm in a
position today and I don't know how much longer

00:37:49.660 --> 00:37:52.400
I'd be in that position to probably get it
done.

00:37:52.400 --> 00:37:54.500
Michael: You could do whatever you want, sure.

00:37:54.500 --> 00:37:55.500
Leonard: Probably, probably.

00:37:55.500 --> 00:38:00.080
I really wish there was some very specific
and wonderful dream project that I've had

00:38:00.080 --> 00:38:05.060
in many, many years and now finally I have
the cache to get it made.

00:38:05.060 --> 00:38:06.060
The answer is no.

00:38:06.060 --> 00:38:09.430
I'm reading a lot of scripts.

00:38:09.430 --> 00:38:16.099
We read several scripts a week in the next
… so they could be made into movies in the

00:38:16.099 --> 00:38:17.410
next 4, 6, 8, 10 months.

00:38:17.410 --> 00:38:18.589
I haven't made a choice yet.

00:38:18.589 --> 00:38:26.240
I have several projects as we say in development,
3 or 4 ideas of mine that are in development

00:38:26.240 --> 00:38:30.570
in various places and in various stages, any
one of which could become a movie or any 2

00:38:30.570 --> 00:38:34.770
or 3 of which could be made into movies in
the next 2 or 3 years, but I don't have 1

00:38:34.770 --> 00:38:35.930
dream all-time project.

00:38:35.930 --> 00:38:36.930
No.

00:38:36.930 --> 00:38:38.369
Michael: Let me ask a more general question
then.

00:38:38.369 --> 00:38:43.349
Is there some overwhelming statement, some
kind of social cause …

00:38:43.349 --> 00:38:45.020
Leonard: Not any one.

00:38:45.020 --> 00:38:46.020
No.

00:38:46.020 --> 00:38:47.020
Not any one.

00:38:47.020 --> 00:38:54.930
I'm at the mercy of what happens to me on
a week-to-week basis, what I read on a week-to-week

00:38:54.930 --> 00:39:02.410
basis, what I'm exposed to, and for that reason,
the development process, I find very painful

00:39:02.410 --> 00:39:09.250
and very difficult because on a given day,
I may read about an idea or see or be told

00:39:09.250 --> 00:39:14.020
about an idea or experience something which
I decide would I really want to make into

00:39:14.020 --> 00:39:19.030
a movie that's a terrific subject and important
and worthwhile, worth making the investment

00:39:19.030 --> 00:39:21.230
and expending the energy, let's do it.

00:39:21.230 --> 00:39:24.810
You go to the studio and say, "Here it is
and this," "Oh, yeah," and they see your passion

00:39:24.810 --> 00:39:26.839
and they see what you're after, they'll say,
"Okay.

00:39:26.839 --> 00:39:29.950
We will put up the money and we'll get a script
written on this subject."

00:39:29.950 --> 00:39:34.579
Now from that point on, it can take several
months till the time you get the story laid

00:39:34.579 --> 00:39:38.349
out, you get the writer you want and he gets
started on or she gets starting on the script,

00:39:38.349 --> 00:39:42.520
and in the meantime, you might go up and do
another movie.

00:39:42.520 --> 00:39:46.220
While you're doing another movie, your life
is changing drastically.

00:39:46.220 --> 00:39:51.660
You are … I am deeply affected by each movie
I make.

00:39:51.660 --> 00:39:56.100
I have literally waken … awaken and find
myself in tears as a result of a scene I'm

00:39:56.100 --> 00:39:58.200
shooting this week in a movie.

00:39:58.200 --> 00:39:59.490
I'm deeply affected by it.

00:39:59.490 --> 00:40:02.380
By the time that picture is finished, I'm
a different person.

00:40:02.380 --> 00:40:06.349
I may be so different that I'm no longer at
all interested in the subject that I just

00:40:06.349 --> 00:40:08.359
put into development 4 or 5 months ago.

00:40:08.359 --> 00:40:10.561
I may look at it and say, "Why didn't I want
to do that?

00:40:10.561 --> 00:40:11.561
It's … that's over.

00:40:11.561 --> 00:40:12.920
That's done.

00:40:12.920 --> 00:40:16.290
I've touched on enough about in this film
or whatever."

00:40:16.290 --> 00:40:19.730
It's a tricky process and I'm new to it.

00:40:19.730 --> 00:40:23.849
It's only been in the last 3 or 4 years that
I've been involved with this particular kind

00:40:23.849 --> 00:40:26.660
of process and I'm learning as I go.

00:40:26.660 --> 00:40:31.510
Michael: Do you feel that on balance if you
look at the body of work that's coming out

00:40:31.510 --> 00:40:41.540
of Hollywood that that work is having an elevating
effect on American society, a negative effect

00:40:41.540 --> 00:40:44.650
or basically is just a relevant entertainment?

00:40:44.650 --> 00:40:49.290
I'm not trying to say choose one of the above,
but how would you describe the impact?

00:40:49.290 --> 00:40:56.720
Leonard: I don't … I can't … Look, I try
to be loyal to my industry and my friends

00:40:56.720 --> 00:40:57.720
in it.

00:40:57.720 --> 00:41:04.420
I cannot honestly say here tonight and say
to you, "We are helping the world with our

00:41:04.420 --> 00:41:05.420
movies.

00:41:05.420 --> 00:41:07.579
We are bettering mankind with our movies.

00:41:07.579 --> 00:41:12.140
We are in touch with all the things that we
should be giving with them and they will … that

00:41:12.140 --> 00:41:13.140
they're being dealt with."

00:41:13.140 --> 00:41:14.319
I can't say that.

00:41:14.319 --> 00:41:16.640
It's … On the other hand, it's too easy
to Hollywood-bash.

00:41:16.640 --> 00:41:24.090
It's always been easy to Hollywood-bash and
to blame Hollywood for our ills.

00:41:24.090 --> 00:41:29.580
I think there's a mutual problem here and
I don't know how well I can express this tonight

00:41:29.580 --> 00:41:30.580
but let's see.

00:41:30.580 --> 00:41:36.400
I do believe that there's a mutuality of failure,
that the audience is not demanding enough

00:41:36.400 --> 00:41:43.670
and … nor are the studios reaching high
enough in terms of the products they want

00:41:43.670 --> 00:41:44.670
to deliver.

00:41:44.670 --> 00:41:52.590
I do believe that audiences do want to … not
to be challenged but to be told what the problem

00:41:52.590 --> 00:41:58.840
is and to be shown a hero or heroine fixing
it, dealing with it, so that in a sense they

00:41:58.840 --> 00:41:59.840
have a … they empathize.

00:41:59.840 --> 00:42:01.270
They have a catharsis.

00:42:01.270 --> 00:42:07.060
They go through the problem without having
dealt with it themselves and come away feeling,

00:42:07.060 --> 00:42:09.480
"Wow, that's … I'm glad that problem is
solved.

00:42:09.480 --> 00:42:11.890
I'm glad … "
Michael: Yes, Spock and Kirk have.

00:42:11.890 --> 00:42:12.890
Leonard: Yeah.

00:42:12.890 --> 00:42:14.010
I'm glad they did that.

00:42:14.010 --> 00:42:19.790
Boy, I feel a lot better about that and it
was fun and that kind of thing.

00:42:19.790 --> 00:42:22.170
I don't think any of us will rise to the occasion
frankly.

00:42:22.170 --> 00:42:25.980
Michael: Do you … Of course and you're right
about the audience.

00:42:25.980 --> 00:42:33.339
Do you feel that the values within our community,
if I may say that, within the filmmaking community,

00:42:33.339 --> 00:42:38.290
within the creative community in the film
industry, that those values accurately reflect

00:42:38.290 --> 00:42:42.220
or reflect in any way the values of society
at large or they had variance somehow?

00:42:42.220 --> 00:42:45.060
Leonard: I think you have an extraordinary
range.

00:42:45.060 --> 00:42:50.500
I think that there are some people in our
industry who are simply want to hit the homerun

00:42:50.500 --> 00:42:58.220
ball every time up if possible and that means
cookie-cutter kind of movies, formula movies,

00:42:58.220 --> 00:43:08.000
movies that do exactly what the audience wants
them to do, and they will be repetitions of

00:43:08.000 --> 00:43:09.000
previous films.

00:43:09.000 --> 00:43:11.040
For example, after 48 Hrs.

00:43:11.040 --> 00:43:16.520
was success … I'll go back to Beverly Hills
Cop, a more obvious choice.

00:43:16.520 --> 00:43:23.760
After Beverly Hills Cop was so successful,
a black gritty Detroit cop comes to clean,

00:43:23.760 --> 00:43:28.359
sanitize Beverly Hills who worked with the
police department there.

00:43:28.359 --> 00:43:32.150
Fish out of water story is what we call it
in our business.

00:43:32.150 --> 00:43:38.150
I must have read a half dozen scripts of cops
who are in the wrong city (laughs) including

00:43:38.150 --> 00:43:46.100
what's on my desk right now of a … 3 cops
who are sheriffs and brothers in Texas who

00:43:46.100 --> 00:43:50.869
go to Monte Carlo.

00:43:50.869 --> 00:43:55.619
I could go on with variations on the scenes
that are being made.

00:43:55.619 --> 00:43:56.619
These movies will be made.

00:43:56.619 --> 00:43:58.400
They've already been … they're being made
all the time.

00:43:58.400 --> 00:44:05.810
Then you got the odd couple buddy cops like
48 Hrs. which was Nick Nolte and Eddie Murphy,

00:44:05.810 --> 00:44:08.070
people who really don't like each other but
are forced to work with each other.

00:44:08.070 --> 00:44:09.330
Then a couple …
Michael: Like Mississippi Burning?

00:44:09.330 --> 00:44:10.330
Leonard: That's right.

00:44:10.330 --> 00:44:11.330
Michael: Same movie?

00:44:11.330 --> 00:44:12.330
Leonard: Exactly, exactly.

00:44:12.330 --> 00:44:14.119
At the end comes a kind of a mutual respect,
you see.

00:44:14.119 --> 00:44:16.190
They discover the values in each other.

00:44:16.190 --> 00:44:19.360
There's just a lot of that stuff going on
and there are people who are going to make

00:44:19.360 --> 00:44:22.160
that stuff because it's … it can … it
will be programmed that way.

00:44:22.160 --> 00:44:24.099
You will get out there and there'll be an
audience who'll come to see those.

00:44:24.099 --> 00:44:25.690
There's very little risk in that kind of stuff.

00:44:25.690 --> 00:44:29.589
Michael: We were talking earlier today about
the most frequently used machine in Hollywood

00:44:29.589 --> 00:44:31.130
obviously is a Xerox machine.

00:44:31.130 --> 00:44:32.280
That's it.

00:44:32.280 --> 00:44:34.570
Two final questions here.

00:44:34.570 --> 00:44:38.481
What do you …
Leonard: To be fair, I didn't say that on

00:44:38.481 --> 00:44:43.490
the other end of the spectrum, there are people
who are doing unique material and dangerous

00:44:43.490 --> 00:44:45.060
financially.

00:44:45.060 --> 00:44:48.410
Sometimes it works, sometimes it doesn't,
but there are people who are committed to

00:44:48.410 --> 00:44:49.410
that kind of work.

00:44:49.410 --> 00:44:52.150
Michael: If I may say so, I think The Good
Mother is a very good example.

00:44:52.150 --> 00:44:56.150
It was not a film that was a safe commercial
bet, by any means.

00:44:56.150 --> 00:44:57.280
Leonard: Yes, it was dangerous.

00:44:57.280 --> 00:44:58.480
Michael: Two last questions.

00:44:58.480 --> 00:45:04.490
Number 1, what do you think it is … you're
doing what almost every actor or actress that

00:45:04.490 --> 00:45:07.540
I know wants to do, what you're doing right
now.

00:45:07.540 --> 00:45:10.890
They would rather be directing, making movies,
shaping movies.

00:45:10.890 --> 00:45:17.140
What do you think it is that it takes for
an actor to make this transition as wonderfully

00:45:17.140 --> 00:45:21.530
successfully as you have done from acting
in front of the camera to serving behind the

00:45:21.530 --> 00:45:22.530
camera?

00:45:22.530 --> 00:45:28.300
Leonard: In my case, I was a lot better prepared
for the job than most people were aware.

00:45:28.300 --> 00:45:32.359
I had already made a sizeable investments
in energy, time, study.

00:45:32.359 --> 00:45:34.740
I had done a lot of theatrical directing.

00:45:34.740 --> 00:45:41.390
I have been on sound stages for almost 40
years in one capacity or another, mostly acting,

00:45:41.390 --> 00:45:42.880
but I had been doing it.

00:45:42.880 --> 00:45:48.599
I had spent a lot of time in editing rooms
on various projects, watching of film go together

00:45:48.599 --> 00:45:50.319
piece by piece from a footage that's been
shot.

00:45:50.319 --> 00:45:57.030
I had thought acting classes for 5 years so
I really was in touch with how to help an

00:45:57.030 --> 00:46:02.010
actor or an actress in a scene.

00:46:02.010 --> 00:46:05.060
I had a lot camera work.

00:46:05.060 --> 00:46:09.050
I had done a lot of my own camera work so
I was very much in touch with lenses and composition

00:46:09.050 --> 00:46:10.050
and so forth.

00:46:10.050 --> 00:46:12.400
I was very well prepared.

00:46:12.400 --> 00:46:15.330
It takes a sense of theatricality.

00:46:15.330 --> 00:46:20.070
It takes a sense of the dynamic of how 1 scene
forms another in film.

00:46:20.070 --> 00:46:24.830
It takes a sense of what to do when a scene
is flat on a sound stage and nothing's happening.

00:46:24.830 --> 00:46:28.940
The actors are doing it but there's fun in,
there's no drama, there's no tension in it.

00:46:28.940 --> 00:46:32.270
They've missed the point perhaps or perhaps
the scene is badly written and needs to be

00:46:32.270 --> 00:46:33.270
rewritten.

00:46:33.270 --> 00:46:35.609
Some sense of the drama.

00:46:35.609 --> 00:46:42.210
For me history of drama helps because the
roots are there whether it's in Beverly Hills

00:46:42.210 --> 00:46:48.819
Cop or whether it's an Oedipus Rex, the roots
are there.

00:46:48.819 --> 00:46:55.330
Directing is a … For me, a great challenge
because it draws on every skill, every sensibility,

00:46:55.330 --> 00:46:58.670
every bit of education you ever had in your
life, drawing all of it.

00:46:58.670 --> 00:47:02.770
Michael: Final question and we turn it over
to the panel group, give you a rough time

00:47:02.770 --> 00:47:05.670
I know.

00:47:05.670 --> 00:47:11.510
Is there one movie that you've seen recently
that you looked at it and you said, "Gee,

00:47:11.510 --> 00:47:12.830
I wish I had taken that script.

00:47:12.830 --> 00:47:17.350
I wish that was a … that was a movie I wish
I wo- had done or could have done."

00:47:17.350 --> 00:47:18.350
Anything that sticks in your mind?

00:47:18.350 --> 00:47:23.700
Leonard: You tend to go to the big hits.

00:47:23.700 --> 00:47:26.670
I'm jealous of Barry Levinson's work.

00:47:26.670 --> 00:47:29.150
I think he's a good filmmaker.

00:47:29.150 --> 00:47:30.390
Michael: He is.

00:47:30.390 --> 00:47:35.390
Leonard: I don’t know if Rain Man is his
best but he certainly … successful in handling

00:47:35.390 --> 00:47:39.330
that material in terms of what the audience
can take with that material.

00:47:39.330 --> 00:47:44.970
I think one can find flaws, one can find fault
with the way that Dustin Hoffman character

00:47:44.970 --> 00:47:50.730
is use almost to the edge of exploitation,
almost.

00:47:50.730 --> 00:47:56.250
His problem is almost exploited but the film
is successful.

00:47:56.250 --> 00:47:57.250
It's good filmmaking.

00:47:57.250 --> 00:47:59.960
On the other hand I'm frankly disappointed
on Mike Nichols.

00:47:59.960 --> 00:48:02.770
I think Working Girl is a good film.

00:48:02.770 --> 00:48:07.170
It's solid all the values there, but I think
that Mike Nichols should do better somehow.

00:48:07.170 --> 00:48:11.930
I don’t think I'm as good a filmmaker ever
will be in terms of social vision as a Mike

00:48:11.930 --> 00:48:15.480
Nichols because has been at it for so long
and he has his own perception and somehow

00:48:15.480 --> 00:48:19.339
I don’t think there's anything unique with
Mike Nichols about that movie.

00:48:19.339 --> 00:48:25.560
It's a successfully made put together film,
good carpentry.

00:48:25.560 --> 00:48:26.560
I don’t know.

00:48:26.560 --> 00:48:29.010
I'd have to do some more thinking about the
films that are out.

00:48:29.010 --> 00:48:36.290
I have not seen, I haven’t seen it so I
don’t know.

00:48:36.290 --> 00:48:38.599
I have … I'm looking forward to that.

00:48:38.599 --> 00:48:41.680
People whose taste I respect tell me that
is a very good picture.

00:48:41.680 --> 00:48:45.869
I don’t have a really good answer to your
question.

00:48:45.869 --> 00:48:47.780
That's the best I can do at the moment.

00:48:47.780 --> 00:48:51.650
Michael: Let me conclude my section of this
by saying that I know that you're always looking

00:48:51.650 --> 00:48:52.670
for material.

00:48:52.670 --> 00:48:57.119
I know that a very inspiring story that you
may want to consider is the Hillsdale College

00:48:57.119 --> 00:49:10.819
Story which is full of … 
It's full of drama, it's full of controversy

00:49:10.819 --> 00:49:12.970
and full of heroes who win.

00:49:12.970 --> 00:49:14.410
Leonard: That's right.

00:49:14.410 --> 00:49:19.369
Michael: With that, having said that, let's
turn it over to Mark and to the panel for

00:49:19.369 --> 00:49:24.750
…
Mark: Good evening ladies and gentlemen.

00:49:24.750 --> 00:49:26.020
My name is Mark Evans.

00:49:26.020 --> 00:49:28.690
I'm moderating the panel this evening.

00:49:28.690 --> 00:49:34.309
I'm a composer and writer and I have a newspaper
column in the New York City Tribune called

00:49:34.309 --> 00:49:36.099
Mark My Words.

00:49:36.099 --> 00:49:41.800
My colleagues on the panel this evening will
include to my left, Chuck Moss, of the Detroit

00:49:41.800 --> 00:49:42.800
News.

00:49:42.800 --> 00:49:48.480
Next to him, Steve Advokat, staff writer for
the Detroit Free Press, and finally, Jim Lyons

00:49:48.480 --> 00:49:51.230
of the Hillsdale Daily News.

00:49:51.230 --> 00:49:57.240
Michael, before going over to Mr. Nimoy, I
might add that apropos your remark about the

00:49:57.240 --> 00:50:02.980
Xerox machine being the most used one in Hollywood,
this has been going on for times even before

00:50:02.980 --> 00:50:04.680
this invention of Xerox.

00:50:04.680 --> 00:50:10.500
Dorothy Parker way back when said the only
ism in Hollywood anyone cares about is plagiarism

00:50:10.500 --> 00:50:12.880
and some of that still true today.

00:50:12.880 --> 00:50:19.050
Mr. Nimoy, the name of this event, this conference
is Popular Entertainment and its impact on

00:50:19.050 --> 00:50:24.750
society and one of the subjects that we've
been pursuing is an interesting one the extent

00:50:24.750 --> 00:50:32.329
to which the values that appear in popular
entertainment represents an influence on our

00:50:32.329 --> 00:50:35.420
society or a reflection of that society.

00:50:35.420 --> 00:50:42.040
There have been certainly cases, a study recently
by the National Institute of Health thy suggested

00:50:42.040 --> 00:50:48.869
that television provides role models for both
parents and children who acknowledge deriving

00:50:48.869 --> 00:50:53.630
much of their sense of values from the entertainment
industry.

00:50:53.630 --> 00:50:58.849
We know that people are interested in what
actors wear, how they behave in their personal

00:50:58.849 --> 00:51:06.619
lives and also the sense of right and wrong,
the sense of meaning, the sense of moral worth

00:51:06.619 --> 00:51:11.070
that appear in films or lack of moral worth
on occasion and there is a running debate

00:51:11.070 --> 00:51:12.070
going on.

00:51:12.070 --> 00:51:14.380
You mentioned those people who are cultural
critics.

00:51:14.380 --> 00:51:19.660
Who place a lot of blame on Hollywood and
on the entertainment industry, there are defenders

00:51:19.660 --> 00:51:24.750
of course of the industry who would say that
the industry is merely reflecting that which

00:51:24.750 --> 00:51:26.920
already exist in society.

00:51:26.920 --> 00:51:31.960
Others would say that the industry is not
just a mirror but it is in fact the primary

00:51:31.960 --> 00:51:34.099
stimulus and a prime mover.

00:51:34.099 --> 00:51:39.319
I've suggested on occasion I'd like to ask
you to comment on this.

00:51:39.319 --> 00:51:43.220
That in fact both realities are in to a certain
extent true.

00:51:43.220 --> 00:51:50.140
That the industry certainly is a tremendous
influence on society but also given a period

00:51:50.140 --> 00:51:55.780
when family has declined and there is something
of a vacuum in our society for other reasons

00:51:55.780 --> 00:52:00.380
people within in the industry are also a part
of our society and reflect the values that

00:52:00.380 --> 00:52:02.720
are evolving within it.

00:52:02.720 --> 00:52:07.480
I'd like to ask you how you see a question
of Hollywood and the entertainment industry

00:52:07.480 --> 00:52:13.190
has either a stimulus or as a reflector and
whether you feel that your view, I'll ask

00:52:13.190 --> 00:52:19.100
this in 2 parts, your view and whether you
feel that your view is shared by the money

00:52:19.100 --> 00:52:25.809
people, the executives, the governing board
if you, decide what pictures are made, which

00:52:25.809 --> 00:52:28.359
networks are going to produce which products.

00:52:28.359 --> 00:52:32.460
That's essentially the essence of the conference
in which we've been engaged and we would appreciate

00:52:32.460 --> 00:52:34.359
very much you sharing us your view of that.

00:52:34.359 --> 00:52:35.359
Leonard: I think you're right.

00:52:35.359 --> 00:52:37.990
I think it operates in both directions.

00:52:37.990 --> 00:52:43.980
I think life imitates art and art imitates
life.

00:52:43.980 --> 00:52:45.410
There's no easy answer here.

00:52:45.410 --> 00:52:47.970
I'm sure we must all accept that.

00:52:47.970 --> 00:52:50.250
You can discuss but there is no easy answer.

00:52:50.250 --> 00:52:55.460
One of the reasons that there's no easy answer
is because Hollywood is not one mind nor is

00:52:55.460 --> 00:52:58.490
television even one mind or motion picture's
one mind.

00:52:58.490 --> 00:53:07.670
There's a very wide range of taste, attitudes,
needs, perceptions and so forth.

00:53:07.670 --> 00:53:16.180
I have a project which I've brought to one
of the networks about 2 years ago and they

00:53:16.180 --> 00:53:21.309
agreed that they would develop and go ahead
with it.

00:53:21.309 --> 00:53:22.309
We have a script.

00:53:22.309 --> 00:53:25.920
I was just told about 2 weeks ago that they've
decided they don’t want to do this project

00:53:25.920 --> 00:53:28.349
and I'm not frankly not sure why.

00:53:28.349 --> 00:53:31.320
It is of a kind subject matter.

00:53:31.320 --> 00:53:36.310
I don’t want too much detail about it, but
I did, having let out the word to some people

00:53:36.310 --> 00:53:38.890
I'm connected with that this project is now
available.

00:53:38.890 --> 00:53:40.072
I'm no longer connected with that particular
network.

00:53:40.072 --> 00:53:46.790
The word comes back to me that network X is
interested in doing some good stuff meaning

00:53:46.790 --> 00:53:52.099
something of social importance, quality.

00:53:52.099 --> 00:53:58.349
That makes a statement about … that I think
speaks to some of these issues.

00:53:58.349 --> 00:54:03.480
What is said is that network Y has chosen
not to but network X is interested in that

00:54:03.480 --> 00:54:04.480
right now.

00:54:04.480 --> 00:54:08.240
Network Y may feel they've enough good stuff
this year and now they're looking for something

00:54:08.240 --> 00:54:13.100
like a Naked Lies starring James Farentino
and a girl who plays a hooker.

00:54:13.100 --> 00:54:17.619
I watch about 5 minutes of that about 10 days
ago.

00:54:17.619 --> 00:54:18.619
I was amazed.

00:54:18.619 --> 00:54:19.619
I don’t watch that kind of stuff.

00:54:19.619 --> 00:54:20.950
I don’t have time frankly.

00:54:20.950 --> 00:54:24.140
It's not a question of taste.

00:54:24.140 --> 00:54:28.380
I don’t have the time, because I do have
this television project I wonder what they're

00:54:28.380 --> 00:54:29.690
buying, what are they doing.

00:54:29.690 --> 00:54:33.980
In the first 3 or 4 minutes I see a judge
played by James Farentino involved with a

00:54:33.980 --> 00:54:42.880
prostitute played by Victoria Principal and
down to his G-string I was amazed at the semi

00:54:42.880 --> 00:54:45.900
nudity, not to his G-string.

00:54:45.900 --> 00:54:50.150
In walks a guy with a camera start shooting
pictures, out comes a gun and the next thing

00:54:50.150 --> 00:54:55.680
you know somebody's dead and the judge is
… Great exposition done, "Bang.

00:54:55.680 --> 00:54:56.680
Bang.

00:54:56.680 --> 00:54:57.680
Bang."

00:54:57.680 --> 00:54:59.640
You got a judge in front with a prostitute
being brought and somebody's dead.

00:54:59.640 --> 00:55:01.850
Mark: All the elements.

00:55:01.850 --> 00:55:02.850
Leonard: Yeah.

00:55:02.850 --> 00:55:03.850
Right.

00:55:03.850 --> 00:55:05.770
It's like 3 minutes and I got that much of
the story out.

00:55:05.770 --> 00:55:09.380
it was amazing storytelling.

00:55:09.380 --> 00:55:10.849
There's that kind of taste.

00:55:10.849 --> 00:55:15.380
They'll probably get very big numbers doing
that kind of thing.

00:55:15.380 --> 00:55:17.210
Why is that?

00:55:17.210 --> 00:55:21.339
Who wants to see … There must be people
who want to see that stuff.

00:55:21.339 --> 00:55:30.589
On the other hand, last night I watch for
quite a while the TV piece about the development

00:55:30.589 --> 00:55:35.890
of the bomb and I don’t know it was sensationalize
or trivialized at all.

00:55:35.890 --> 00:55:40.690
There was remarkably in-depth study of all
these characters and their motivations and

00:55:40.690 --> 00:55:44.089
the politics of the case and the science of
the case.

00:55:44.089 --> 00:55:49.760
We're looking at a very wide range of stuff
and I can't tell you, I'm not an authority

00:55:49.760 --> 00:55:55.390
on what the numbers were last night as compared
to Naked Lie with James Farentino, I don’t

00:55:55.390 --> 00:55:56.390
know.

00:55:56.390 --> 00:55:59.369
One would hope that there are a lot of people
watching last night and really interested

00:55:59.369 --> 00:56:01.980
but I can't guarantee that.

00:56:01.980 --> 00:56:07.720
There are a lot of different kinds of work
being done for a lot of different reasons

00:56:07.720 --> 00:56:12.829
and at a different time and I do believe that
there are times when you can go to a network

00:56:12.829 --> 00:56:19.000
and say, "I have an idea and I can … I can
show why this idea has merit on basis of the

00:56:19.000 --> 00:56:23.131
numbers because X number of millions of people
are involved in this kind of activity and

00:56:23.131 --> 00:56:25.360
this is the film about that activity so they
will be interested."

00:56:25.360 --> 00:56:27.200
And that wasn’t bad on the one hand.

00:56:27.200 --> 00:56:37.960
On the other hand, there may be some new idea
that is bubbling on the edge of social consciousness

00:56:37.960 --> 00:56:41.980
that you could take to a network or to a studio
and they might say, "Yeah, you're right.

00:56:41.980 --> 00:56:43.920
I think that's an idea whose time is coming.

00:56:43.920 --> 00:56:45.960
Perhaps it's not yet come but it's coming.

00:56:45.960 --> 00:56:46.960
Let's investigate that.

00:56:46.960 --> 00:56:47.960
Let's explore to do it."

00:56:47.960 --> 00:56:49.069
I think there are all kinds.

00:56:49.069 --> 00:56:53.600
I think the individual finally has to decide
for him or herself which way do I want to

00:56:53.600 --> 00:56:54.600
go?

00:56:54.600 --> 00:56:55.600
Why am I in this thing?

00:56:55.600 --> 00:56:58.099
Michael: I think one of the interesting things
to observe here, you'll pardon me, is that

00:56:58.099 --> 00:57:04.339
the series that Mr. Nimoy is talking about,
about the development of the atomic bomb was

00:57:04.339 --> 00:57:10.119
produced by Aaron Spelling who has given the
world Charlie's Angels, the Love Boat and

00:57:10.119 --> 00:57:15.050
other important contributions to western civilization
in the past.

00:57:15.050 --> 00:57:19.960
Sometimes, quality product from surprising
and unexpected sources.

00:57:19.960 --> 00:57:26.210
Speaker 4: Mr. Nimoy, I'm going to drop the
dirty boot and mention the science fiction

00:57:26.210 --> 00:57:30.579
angle, hopefully, without inducing you to
jump off the podium and begin throttling me.

00:57:30.579 --> 00:57:38.680
I'm 35 years old, I remember growing up in
the 50s and 60s and one of the staples of

00:57:38.680 --> 00:57:46.630
popular culture was the world of the future
was this confident bright happy future a better

00:57:46.630 --> 00:57:52.609
world which we would inevitably create generally
by this far future date of around 1979.

00:57:52.609 --> 00:57:55.970
Now, here it is.

00:57:55.970 --> 00:57:56.970
It's 1989.

00:57:56.970 --> 00:58:00.990
This must be the world of future and yet as
you said, "Here we are and all the … all

00:58:00.990 --> 00:58:04.280
the audience seems to want is reassurance."

00:58:04.280 --> 00:58:05.329
As you said, "Certainty."

00:58:05.329 --> 00:58:10.900
You want the good mother to confront the situation
and win or fight nobly.

00:58:10.900 --> 00:58:16.930
The audience it all seemed to be any images
anymore of a confident better world, at least

00:58:16.930 --> 00:58:23.390
I don’t see very many of them, even in the
Star Trek extrapolation, it's kind of fear

00:58:23.390 --> 00:58:25.309
of ecological disaster.

00:58:25.309 --> 00:58:30.750
My question is, what does that say about the
condition of the country or the mentally of

00:58:30.750 --> 00:58:39.730
people today that instead of a confident vision
of where we're going, people want reassurance.

00:58:39.730 --> 00:58:47.099
Leonard: We've been through some devastating
times I think as a civilization in the last

00:58:47.099 --> 00:58:50.290
couple of decades.

00:58:50.290 --> 00:58:58.850
I think we've been shown the frailty of the
society and the planet and I think we're looking

00:58:58.850 --> 00:59:06.530
for some kind of reassurance that both will
survive.

00:59:06.530 --> 00:59:11.150
When I was in the research that I did for
Star Trek 4, I talked to a lot of scientist

00:59:11.150 --> 00:59:18.569
about their concerns or expectations or hopes
for immediate future.

00:59:18.569 --> 00:59:24.339
What exciting breakthroughs you think might
happen in the next 10 or 15 years?

00:59:24.339 --> 00:59:27.580
What great problems do you foresee in the
next 10 or 15 years?

00:59:27.580 --> 00:59:31.890
I had a wide range of answers including a
very highly respected scientist who told me

00:59:31.890 --> 00:59:35.839
that he expects that we will make contact
with another intelligence by the end of the

00:59:35.839 --> 00:59:42.480
century and I was quite startled by that and
he's very serious about it.

00:59:42.480 --> 00:59:44.010
I wonder whether that's good or bad.

00:59:44.010 --> 00:59:45.450
I don’t know how we'll handle that.

00:59:45.450 --> 00:59:47.589
Speaker 4: Do you think they're getting our
television up there?

00:59:47.589 --> 00:59:54.240
Leonard: I don’t know.

00:59:54.240 --> 01:00:03.119
My point is this, that science is a concern
for example that there is a feeling that things

01:00:03.119 --> 01:00:06.680
aren’t good with the planet.

01:00:06.680 --> 01:00:12.059
There's a sense that we cannot deal with all
of the problems simultaneously that therefore

01:00:12.059 --> 01:00:17.220
there's a question of priority that there's
the politics of the situation and the economics

01:00:17.220 --> 01:00:24.079
of the situation that the politicians have
to decide at some point, yes, there's enough

01:00:24.079 --> 01:00:27.530
… I have enough of a mandate from my constituents
that they want that fixed.

01:00:27.530 --> 01:00:31.240
They want that problem stopped and if we announced
that we're going to spend a lot of money to

01:00:31.240 --> 01:00:37.059
fix it, my people will go for it because they
feel we've gone as far down the road that

01:00:37.059 --> 01:00:38.059
we can with this.

01:00:38.059 --> 01:00:42.710
The scientist are concerned in a given day
a problem that they have been talking about,

01:00:42.710 --> 01:00:48.550
complaining about, concern about will reach
a point, will reach that point where the citizenry

01:00:48.550 --> 01:00:49.710
says, "Fix it."

01:00:49.710 --> 01:00:51.650
The politicians will say, "Right."

01:00:51.650 --> 01:00:54.160
And they’ll turn to the scientists and say,
"Fix it."

01:00:54.160 --> 01:00:57.280
The scientists have to say, "We can't anymore.

01:00:57.280 --> 01:00:58.849
It's gone too far.

01:00:58.849 --> 01:00:59.990
We don’t have the means right now.

01:00:59.990 --> 01:01:03.060
We can start working with the money you gave
or whatever."

01:01:03.060 --> 01:01:10.940
You get the point that the prioritizing of
our concerns has become a concern and there

01:01:10.940 --> 01:01:12.109
is a sense of uneasiness.

01:01:12.109 --> 01:01:16.500
You're reading the newspaper suddenly that
there's a ozone problem.

01:01:16.500 --> 01:01:24.820
That the layer is disappearing rapidly and
now there's a report that there's a major

01:01:24.820 --> 01:01:26.720
international conference which says, "Yes.

01:01:26.720 --> 01:01:27.720
We agree.

01:01:27.720 --> 01:01:28.890
Let's fix it."

01:01:28.890 --> 01:01:35.569
It feels like a band-aid to me like just in
time, just before the patient gets seriously

01:01:35.569 --> 01:01:38.640
ill and perhaps unredeemable ill.

01:01:38.640 --> 01:01:40.859
I personally feel that way.

01:01:40.859 --> 01:01:41.859
I have that concern.

01:01:41.859 --> 01:01:44.460
I don’t have that sense of, "Oh, boy.

01:01:44.460 --> 01:01:45.549
We can do anything."

01:01:45.549 --> 01:01:57.299
Having lost a war for the first time, I think
our citizens feel less than unbeatable obviously.

01:01:57.299 --> 01:02:01.799
These are concerns that I think have affected
all of us and maybe in all fairness to our

01:02:01.799 --> 01:02:04.670
society that's why we want to be told, "Hey,
everything's okay.

01:02:04.670 --> 01:02:05.670
Go see a movie.

01:02:05.670 --> 01:02:07.380
Enjoy yourself and watch they'll probably
be fixed.

01:02:07.380 --> 01:02:08.380
Go home feeling better.

01:02:08.380 --> 01:02:13.010
Don’t take the problem along with you, we'll
fix it at the theater for you."

01:02:13.010 --> 01:02:15.890
Speaker 4: the 1930s.

01:02:15.890 --> 01:02:17.329
Leonard: Yeah.

01:02:17.329 --> 01:02:18.329
Exactly.

01:02:18.329 --> 01:02:23.719
It is historical that when times are bad films
do well particularly escapist films, when

01:02:23.719 --> 01:02:26.780
people have problems they want to go see a
movie and be made to feel good.

01:02:26.780 --> 01:02:29.780
Speaker 5: Good evening Mr. Nimoy.

01:02:29.780 --> 01:02:32.119
Leonard: Have I depressed all of you?

01:02:32.119 --> 01:02:37.920
Speaker 5: There was a woman recently from
a Detroit suburb sat down with her 3 young

01:02:37.920 --> 01:02:41.140
children who watched Married with Children
was incensed by it

01:02:41.140 --> 01:02:46.070
Leonard: I have to plead ignorance because
I have never seen this show but go ahead.

01:02:46.070 --> 01:02:47.070
Speaker 5: It's about.

01:02:47.070 --> 01:02:50.980
She sent her children out and she watched
it and it's an adult comedy on the Fox Network

01:02:50.980 --> 01:02:55.520
and she thought of the one woman led a writing
campaign to get that show and others that

01:02:55.520 --> 01:02:58.510
she finds objectionable off the air.

01:02:58.510 --> 01:03:03.549
Last year Martin Scorsese has released a film
about Jesus Christ that offended many people

01:03:03.549 --> 01:03:06.710
who picketed the film throughout its theatrical
run.

01:03:06.710 --> 01:03:13.970
My question is, how do you balance making
projects which on the one hand may offend

01:03:13.970 --> 01:03:17.099
people with at the same time trying to raise
consciousness?

01:03:17.099 --> 01:03:19.880
Are there any topics that Hollywood just should
not touch?

01:03:19.880 --> 01:03:21.740
Leonard: No.

01:03:21.740 --> 01:03:23.089
I don’t think so.

01:03:23.089 --> 01:03:27.130
I don’t think there's any topic that should
be taboo, censored, you don’t talk about

01:03:27.130 --> 01:03:30.670
that or deal with it.

01:03:30.670 --> 01:03:36.700
Particularly in television and films the individual
has the right not to buy a ticket, not to

01:03:36.700 --> 01:03:39.650
go see it, turn in to another channel and
I believe that.

01:03:39.650 --> 01:03:43.599
I think it's an easy out and I think some
people take it as an easy out but I think

01:03:43.599 --> 01:03:44.599
it happens to be true.

01:03:44.599 --> 01:03:45.670
You don’t have to buy that book.

01:03:45.670 --> 01:03:46.670
You don’t have to read that book.

01:03:46.670 --> 01:03:49.270
You don’t have to watch that TV show.

01:03:49.270 --> 01:03:56.329
The question is at what point do you have
the right to prevent others from seeing, hearing,

01:03:56.329 --> 01:03:59.770
experiencing that thing that you find objectionable?

01:03:59.770 --> 01:04:06.210
Society sets standards and there are community
standards as well and in some communities

01:04:06.210 --> 01:04:07.589
the standards are different than others.

01:04:07.589 --> 01:04:09.059
I think that's valid.

01:04:09.059 --> 01:04:12.829
You establish a community where you chose
your community, you're entitled to have some

01:04:12.829 --> 01:04:16.549
sense of expectations on what the experience
is going to be within that community and if

01:04:16.549 --> 01:04:21.500
you don’t like it, you move it to another
community but …

01:04:21.500 --> 01:04:27.420
We're still a democracy and must be and I
think that's important that somebody have

01:04:27.420 --> 01:04:32.171
the right speak out on an issue as they see
fit and nobody has to listen if they don’t

01:04:32.171 --> 01:04:33.171
want to.

01:04:33.171 --> 01:04:37.819
It's difficult, I sympathize with both sides
but I finally come down with the side of they

01:04:37.819 --> 01:04:38.819
must allowed to do it.

01:04:38.819 --> 01:04:39.819
That's my sense of it.

01:04:39.819 --> 01:04:42.119
Michael: Follow up question on that.

01:04:42.119 --> 01:04:46.920
Would you be interested if someone approached
you to direct the movie version of the Satanic

01:04:46.920 --> 01:04:47.920
Verses?

01:04:47.920 --> 01:04:55.710
Leonard: I don’t think so.

01:04:55.710 --> 01:04:57.890
That's a sad terrible problem.

01:04:57.890 --> 01:04:59.160
Michael: It's terrible.

01:04:59.160 --> 01:05:02.549
That's awful, yeah, but …
Leonard: Terrible problem.

01:05:02.549 --> 01:05:03.549
No.

01:05:03.549 --> 01:05:04.869
The answer is no.

01:05:04.869 --> 01:05:09.310
Michael: Because of concerns for your own
safety or because of concerns for …

01:05:09.310 --> 01:05:17.040
Leonard: I'd say the first place I haven’t
read it and when I do read it, if I do, it's

01:05:17.040 --> 01:05:22.230
going to take me quite a while to understand
what it means to those people who are offended

01:05:22.230 --> 01:05:23.349
by it.

01:05:23.349 --> 01:05:27.940
I may have … I will have an entirely different
reactions to it than they and I will have

01:05:27.940 --> 01:05:34.380
to … I would have to make an intense investigation
of what concerns them before I … in order

01:05:34.380 --> 01:05:37.809
to make an intelligent decision about whether
or not to do it.

01:05:37.809 --> 01:05:39.760
Interesting way if I may say so.

01:05:39.760 --> 01:05:43.740
I find it fascinating that Mississippi Burning
was made by an Englishman, Alan Parker.

01:05:43.740 --> 01:05:45.810
I don’t know where he was in the 60s.

01:05:45.810 --> 01:05:48.329
I was here, you see.

01:05:48.329 --> 01:05:52.570
I don’t know where he was and maybe that's
the way it had to happen.

01:05:52.570 --> 01:05:56.609
Somebody who was from thousands of miles away
and from a different society, different … was

01:05:56.609 --> 01:06:00.410
not emotional involved, subjective and physically
involved, maybe he could make it, I couldn’t.

01:06:00.410 --> 01:06:03.829
Michael: Well see, my rather flipping question
is getting into an important point.

01:06:03.829 --> 01:06:08.880
You would then agree that there are certain
kinds of material that would be so deeply

01:06:08.880 --> 01:06:12.380
offensive to some people that you would decline
to participate?

01:06:12.380 --> 01:06:14.880
Leonard: Why did he rush to write it?

01:06:14.880 --> 01:06:15.880
That's the question.

01:06:15.880 --> 01:06:18.539
Michael: Well that's his prob- I mean I'm
not sure.

01:06:18.539 --> 01:06:20.040
Leonard: If it is his problem, yes, you're
right.

01:06:20.040 --> 01:06:21.040
Michael: Major problem.

01:06:21.040 --> 01:06:24.520
Leonard: It is a problem but what I'm saying
is that maybe there's some passionate filmmaker

01:06:24.520 --> 01:06:27.130
who believes in that particular piece of material.

01:06:27.130 --> 01:06:32.020
You have the right … I think … I'm really
speaking out of ignorance here, I haven’t

01:06:32.020 --> 01:06:36.599
read the material and I don’t know … obviously
some people are deeply, deeply offended by

01:06:36.599 --> 01:06:37.599
it.

01:06:37.599 --> 01:06:38.599
Michael: I would say so.

01:06:38.599 --> 01:06:49.289
Leonard: Including Cat Stevens, but, there's
an interesting incident in LA, have you heard

01:06:49.289 --> 01:06:50.289
about it?

01:06:50.289 --> 01:06:52.609
Leonard: About Jeff Edwards, you know about
that?

01:06:52.609 --> 01:06:53.609
Michael: Yes.

01:06:53.609 --> 01:07:00.400
Leonard: There's a TV-radio personality in
LA on a particular radio station who is one

01:07:00.400 --> 01:07:07.250
of this garbage kind of guys who in order
to get some heat going has decided that he

01:07:07.250 --> 01:07:10.660
was going to have all his listeners send in
their Cat Stevens records and they would burn

01:07:10.660 --> 01:07:14.710
all this material and there was going to be
a public burning of Cat Stevens record because

01:07:14.710 --> 01:07:21.730
Cat Stevens agreed that that Rushti should
die and one of his colleagues on the station

01:07:21.730 --> 01:07:28.610
has a talk show and on that colleague's talk
show there was a promo for this other talk

01:07:28.610 --> 01:07:34.480
show host record burning and his colleague
Jeff Edwards refused to have it aired on his

01:07:34.480 --> 01:07:39.210
spot because he said that's terrorism and
I won't have it.

01:07:39.210 --> 01:07:45.340
I won't promote his burning of records even
though I agree that Cat Stevens is wrong.

01:07:45.340 --> 01:07:47.170
He had to leave the station.

01:07:47.170 --> 01:07:48.750
Gave up his job at the station.

01:07:48.750 --> 01:07:51.500
Well that's a question of personal choice.

01:07:51.500 --> 01:07:57.030
Jim: Jim Lyons from the Daily News.

01:07:57.030 --> 01:08:00.100
Thank you for coming tonight by the way.

01:08:00.100 --> 01:08:03.369
Now we used to sit down with Catholics a little
bit in this country.

01:08:03.369 --> 01:08:08.579
We used not see their way as a general population.

01:08:08.579 --> 01:08:09.810
There was the wasp view.

01:08:09.810 --> 01:08:15.099
Now, we are seeing that we have a very mixed
country and Rushti is a good case of it.

01:08:15.099 --> 01:08:20.050
we are not looking at his book from the point
of view of a man who is breaking away from

01:08:20.050 --> 01:08:25.489
his religion as many people have done here
in the United States.

01:08:25.489 --> 01:08:30.980
I was wondering, we were talking earlier and
I was listening to these guys this big important

01:08:30.980 --> 01:08:40.529
news men that we've got Clint Eastwood and
we've got all of the white male leads who

01:08:40.529 --> 01:08:47.170
do fantastic things, never get shot or if
they do they struggle through, but we just

01:08:47.170 --> 01:08:56.480
don’t seem to be able to produce a hero
or a real survivor for many other race.

01:08:56.480 --> 01:09:00.480
It seems we've narrowed our point of view
through television.

01:09:00.480 --> 01:09:02.250
I don’t know … and through movies.

01:09:02.250 --> 01:09:04.210
Michael: I'm sorry to jump in.

01:09:04.210 --> 01:09:08.380
What would you call Eddie Murphy?

01:09:08.380 --> 01:09:11.109
What would you call Danny Glover in Lethal
Weapon?

01:09:11.109 --> 01:09:16.880
Jim: I think we've integrated in a lot of
ways but I also think a lot of it has been

01:09:16.880 --> 01:09:17.880
token.

01:09:17.880 --> 01:09:22.929
I think Eddie Murphy just loves to … He's
a different character.

01:09:22.929 --> 01:09:27.029
He's always enjoyed that playing with the
white guys head.

01:09:27.029 --> 01:09:28.890
Michael: Does pretty well with it.

01:09:28.890 --> 01:09:30.170
Jim: Yeah.

01:09:30.170 --> 01:09:37.210
Leonard: I think the answer is … I'm not
sure you're absolutely accurate really.

01:09:37.210 --> 01:09:38.620
I understand the question.

01:09:38.620 --> 01:09:42.230
The question has validity but I'm not sure
it is really, really true.

01:09:42.230 --> 01:09:49.830
I think, again, it feels like those guys out
there can't do anything but make a white man

01:09:49.830 --> 01:09:50.830
hero.

01:09:50.830 --> 01:09:51.830
I'm not sure that's entirely true.

01:09:51.830 --> 01:09:53.250
I'm very curious about this.

01:09:53.250 --> 01:10:01.620
I've just received a project, an offer of
a project called Judge D. There in Chinese

01:10:01.620 --> 01:10:06.640
history a very famous judge who was a kind
of a Sherlock Holmes detective on his own,

01:10:06.640 --> 01:10:09.050
who went out and investigated his own cases.

01:10:09.050 --> 01:10:13.250
Very famous in Chinese history and there was
been some books written about him and I have

01:10:13.250 --> 01:10:19.290
been submitted this project and I say to myself
… Either acting or direct.

01:10:19.290 --> 01:10:26.950
I said to myself, "Do we … Do we want to
see a Chinese judge in China in the period

01:10:26.950 --> 01:10:32.420
1600s or whatever investigating cases?"

01:10:32.420 --> 01:10:38.050
I think the answer is if it's well done, yes,
but one has to stop and wonder.

01:10:38.050 --> 01:10:39.050
I don’t know.

01:10:39.050 --> 01:10:40.050
I don’t have the answer to that question.

01:10:40.050 --> 01:10:44.230
I do think that we've had … There are exceptions
to your rule though.

01:10:44.230 --> 01:10:46.290
Not a lot, but there are some.

01:10:46.290 --> 01:10:48.280
Michael: That raises another interesting question.

01:10:48.280 --> 01:10:56.121
You've obviously played people from another
planet, a person from another planet, would

01:10:56.121 --> 01:11:00.880
you feel … You were offered this to act
in, would you feel comfortable doing a makeup

01:11:00.880 --> 01:11:04.280
transition in playing an Asian …
Leonard: Physically comfortable, no.

01:11:04.280 --> 01:11:05.280
I wouldn’t.

01:11:05.280 --> 01:11:07.480
Michael: But would you feel comfortable as
a …

01:11:07.480 --> 01:11:09.292
Leonard: It's a good question.

01:11:09.292 --> 01:11:11.000
Michael: … a white person impersonating
an Asian person?

01:11:11.000 --> 01:11:12.000
Leonard: Good question.

01:11:12.000 --> 01:11:14.080
There is also a project called Black Elk Speaks.

01:11:14.080 --> 01:11:22.350
With Black Elk is … I'm just getting to
know a very revered figure in Indian history.

01:11:22.350 --> 01:11:30.300
He is … He was a religious leader of the
Indian people and there's a play called Black

01:11:30.300 --> 01:11:34.070
Elk Speaks and there was a script for a film
called Black Elk Speaks and have been asked

01:11:34.070 --> 01:11:39.590
to play the lead and I had this intense discussion
with the people who brought it to me who claimed

01:11:39.590 --> 01:11:41.640
to speak for the Sioux Nation.

01:11:41.640 --> 01:11:43.460
Who claimed the Sioux Nation said, "Yes.

01:11:43.460 --> 01:11:45.679
If he plays it that will sit well with us."

01:11:45.679 --> 01:11:46.679
He's saying.

01:11:46.679 --> 01:11:50.770
Now, I find that very flattering but I don’t
feel too terrific about it.

01:11:50.770 --> 01:11:51.770
I have played Indian.

01:11:51.770 --> 01:11:54.119
I don’t know if this audience is aware.

01:11:54.119 --> 01:11:55.119
I played Indian.

01:11:55.119 --> 01:12:02.790
I played Indians in Gun Smoke, in Wagon Train,
in a number of television westerns and even

01:12:02.790 --> 01:12:05.140
in some films.

01:12:05.140 --> 01:12:11.780
Up until the early 60s and somebody raised
my consciousness and said, "Hey, that's too

01:12:11.780 --> 01:12:12.780
terrific.

01:12:12.780 --> 01:12:17.120
You know there are Indians who are out there
who'd like to have the job."

01:12:17.120 --> 01:12:19.180
Maybe do a better job of representing their
raise than you are.

01:12:19.180 --> 01:12:25.630
As a matter fact, the very first job that
I had as an Indian was in 19- Don’t laugh.

01:12:25.630 --> 01:12:26.630
Michael: I'm sorry.

01:12:26.630 --> 01:12:28.010
It's just in the phrase.

01:12:28.010 --> 01:12:29.630
My first job as an Indian.

01:12:29.630 --> 01:12:30.630
Leonard: Yes.

01:12:30.630 --> 01:12:31.630
It was in 1950.

01:12:31.630 --> 01:12:36.200
It was in a picture called the Old Overland
Trail with Rex Allen, the Arizona Cowboy.

01:12:36.200 --> 01:12:44.041
His wonder horse Coco and the Indian was a
savage Indian that I was asked to play and

01:12:44.041 --> 01:12:47.980
the reason I got the job was because it had
been offered to Iron Eyes Cody, an Indian

01:12:47.980 --> 01:12:52.000
actor who refused to play it, and I got the
job.

01:12:52.000 --> 01:12:54.960
I wasn’t thinking about, "Well, gee, I shouldn’t
be playing nasty Indian."

01:12:54.960 --> 01:12:57.820
Michael: It's better than playing the wonder
horse, Coco.

01:12:57.820 --> 01:13:04.670
Mark: We have an additional member of our
panel who arrived a few minutes late will

01:13:04.670 --> 01:13:09.070
ask you, sir, please identify yourself and
ask your question and that's not supposed

01:13:09.070 --> 01:13:12.300
to sound like a message that would be coming
out of an congressional committee.

01:13:12.300 --> 01:13:13.400
You're welcome here.

01:13:13.400 --> 01:13:16.900
Mike: Mr. Nimoy, I'm Mike Rosenbaum from Detroit
Jewish News.

01:13:16.900 --> 01:13:22.400
I want to get back to something you mentioned
before about how deeply you were affected

01:13:22.400 --> 01:13:24.690
by the idea of directing certain scenes.

01:13:24.690 --> 01:13:30.060
Did you go through the same process as an
actor and if not, why not?

01:13:30.060 --> 01:13:31.060
Leonard: Yes.

01:13:31.060 --> 01:13:34.739
I do go the same process as an actor.

01:13:34.739 --> 01:13:38.760
I tend to assimilate the condition that I'm
dealing with.

01:13:38.760 --> 01:13:46.010
It tends to become part of me, I live with
it.

01:13:46.010 --> 01:13:53.360
On one of these jobs you're on it maybe 12
hours a day physically, maybe 16, 18 hours

01:13:53.360 --> 01:13:57.620
a day mentally and I can't help to be affected
by it.

01:13:57.620 --> 01:14:03.130
I'm in a much better mood when I'm doing comedy
than I am doing when I'm doing tragedy.

01:14:03.130 --> 01:14:08.080
I was aware … Particularly, when we're making
the Star Trek series where we went months

01:14:08.080 --> 01:14:13.670
at a time, many months at a time of shooting
on the films which it's 10 weeks and you're

01:14:13.670 --> 01:14:19.489
done, but particularly on series I was conscious
of the fact that on Saturdays I would still

01:14:19.489 --> 01:14:22.740
be in the Spock mode on a day off.

01:14:22.740 --> 01:14:27.960
Sunday afternoon I would start to relax a
little bit, a little loose but on Saturdays

01:14:27.960 --> 01:14:35.719
I was still quite rigid and I was still doing
Spock in my personal life, for better or worse.

01:14:35.719 --> 01:14:39.780
Michael: It must have been a lot of fun for
your family.

01:14:39.780 --> 01:14:41.060
Leonard: No.

01:14:41.060 --> 01:14:44.890
Yes, it does happen to me.

01:14:44.890 --> 01:14:47.350
I get deeply immersed in the process, yeah.

01:14:47.350 --> 01:14:51.520
Mike: As a director, do you get immersed in
the different characters maybe on different

01:14:51.520 --> 01:14:54.340
days depending what scene you're going to
shoot or do you identify with one character?

01:14:54.340 --> 01:14:55.340
Leonard: No.

01:14:55.340 --> 01:15:00.850
As a director, I think my connection is with
the overview.

01:15:00.850 --> 01:15:03.830
It's not with anyone particular character
at any one time.

01:15:03.830 --> 01:15:08.060
It's with the sense what the nature of the
material is.

01:15:08.060 --> 01:15:15.400
It's been widely reported I told a reported
about it 3 or 4 months ago and it was widely

01:15:15.400 --> 01:15:22.659
printed that during the making of The Good
Mother, I woke up one night in tears and grabbed

01:15:22.659 --> 01:15:26.989
for a piece of paper and a pencil and I wrote
down 3 words and the words were it's about

01:15:26.989 --> 01:15:27.989
lost.

01:15:27.989 --> 01:15:35.489
I had gone through some very personal loses
around that time not long before that and

01:15:35.489 --> 01:15:38.420
I suddenly realized that that was what this
film was putting me in touch with, was my

01:15:38.420 --> 01:15:42.719
own sense of loss so … It was a very moving
experience for me.

01:15:42.719 --> 01:15:47.929
On the other hand I felt good about the fact
that I was so much in touch with the process

01:15:47.929 --> 01:15:51.300
and aware of the fact that I was in touched
with the process.

01:15:51.300 --> 01:15:52.900
It's a creative life.

01:15:52.900 --> 01:15:56.400
Mark: I know that we have some questions from
members of our audience.

01:15:56.400 --> 01:16:02.440
Again, we should remind you that we are videotaping
this and in order to hear your question, we'll

01:16:02.440 --> 01:16:06.929
ask you to please raise your hand but wait
until someone approaches you with the microphone.

01:16:06.929 --> 01:16:10.980
If you ask your question and no one is near
you with the microphone, we won't be able

01:16:10.980 --> 01:16:15.830
to hear you or if we if we hear you, it won't
be preserved for posterity on videotape.

01:16:15.830 --> 01:16:20.739
Please raise your hand and wait and someone
will be over with a mic.

01:16:20.739 --> 01:16:30.010
Speaker 8: I'm curious about your feelings
toward the Walt Disney company with respect

01:16:30.010 --> 01:16:35.340
to their rather unique image in Hollywood
of … or to the public of high morals and

01:16:35.340 --> 01:16:36.340
ethics.

01:16:36.340 --> 01:16:40.090
Secondly, what is attributed to their great
success in comparison to ...

01:16:40.090 --> 01:16:41.840
Leonard: The Disney Company.

01:16:41.840 --> 01:16:48.409
That's a multi-level question really.

01:16:48.409 --> 01:16:54.659
The people who are now managing the Disney
Company, the people I deal with—current

01:16:54.659 --> 01:16:59.679
management, were the management of Paramount
when we were making the first 3 Star Trek

01:16:59.679 --> 01:17:04.550
films there, Eisner and Katzenberg with the
people who actually hired me to the direct

01:17:04.550 --> 01:17:08.390
Star Trek 3 and hired me to direct Star Trek
4.

01:17:08.390 --> 01:17:15.199
Then they left while we were making the Star
Trek 4 and they became the management of Disney.

01:17:15.199 --> 01:17:19.239
They hired me to do Three Men and a Baby and
The Good Mother.

01:17:19.239 --> 01:17:22.630
Now, they're extremely hardworking.

01:17:22.630 --> 01:17:25.750
You asked about their success.

01:17:25.750 --> 01:17:26.750
They're extremely hardworking.

01:17:26.750 --> 01:17:28.940
They're very bright.

01:17:28.940 --> 01:17:33.350
I think they have a great sense of audience,
great sense of an audience.

01:17:33.350 --> 01:17:39.190
They are trying to broaden the nature of their
product in films.

01:17:39.190 --> 01:17:43.770
The Good Mother was, I think, probably the
first step in a direction to get away from

01:17:43.770 --> 01:17:49.260
a certain kind of movie, get away from ... and
identification with a certain predictable

01:17:49.260 --> 01:17:52.010
kind of movie and a movie and some other kind
of territory.

01:17:52.010 --> 01:17:57.190
Not successful unfortunately and I just hope
it doesn't cause him to pull in their wings

01:17:57.190 --> 01:18:03.940
and go back to the more reliable kind of product.

01:18:03.940 --> 01:18:08.940
On another level, it's Michael Eisner who
is now in charge of all the Disney Parks and

01:18:08.940 --> 01:18:11.840
so forth and that sort of thing.

01:18:11.840 --> 01:18:19.511
That's an incredibly successful, incredibly
popular part of our culture and not just the

01:18:19.511 --> 01:18:21.290
American culture but the world culture.

01:18:21.290 --> 01:18:28.380
They're opening parks in France and with great
success and Mickey Mouse has just been to

01:18:28.380 --> 01:18:35.409
China and Russia and being well received wherever
it goes and it's an incredible thing to me

01:18:35.409 --> 01:18:37.190
to be around that.

01:18:37.190 --> 01:18:45.720
I went last week to Disneyland in Southern
California because there was a program for

01:18:45.720 --> 01:18:51.470
the families of the Challenger Shuttle that
was destroyed in an accident and they were

01:18:51.470 --> 01:18:56.530
being honored and may have started up an educational
program in connection with the Challenger

01:18:56.530 --> 01:19:01.090
and they were there and had asked me to come
and introduce them.

01:19:01.090 --> 01:19:02.429
I went through the park.

01:19:02.429 --> 01:19:08.489
I haven't been to the park at some time and
I'm in awe of that kind of commonality of

01:19:08.489 --> 01:19:16.969
sensibility of entertainment, what people
want to see, what people enjoy.

01:19:16.969 --> 01:19:18.050
There's no question about it.

01:19:18.050 --> 01:19:19.470
They have something that people want.

01:19:19.470 --> 01:19:22.820
They're in touch with something that people
want and will flock to see.

01:19:22.820 --> 01:19:26.710
There's a magic about it that draws people
like a magnet.

01:19:26.710 --> 01:19:27.940
Incredible.

01:19:27.940 --> 01:19:34.460
Walt Disney did have that sensibility, that
sense of taste.

01:19:34.460 --> 01:19:45.440
Speaker 9: Considering the fact that Star
Trek was cancelled and then went on syndication

01:19:45.440 --> 01:19:53.080
to become one of the most popular television
series of all time and that we see more and

01:19:53.080 --> 01:19:59.110
more people turning to public television and
videotapes and cable these days, do you think

01:19:59.110 --> 01:20:05.080
that the networks are doing justice to the
American public in the context of an entertainment

01:20:05.080 --> 01:20:06.080
industry?

01:20:06.080 --> 01:20:07.920
Leonard: Help me.

01:20:07.920 --> 01:20:09.770
How do you put Star Trek in a context?

01:20:09.770 --> 01:20:12.489
Where does it fit in the question?

01:20:12.489 --> 01:20:19.510
Speaker 9: The fact that it gained such great
popularity after it was cancelled.

01:20:19.510 --> 01:20:25.130
Leonard: I don't think they knew what we were.

01:20:25.130 --> 01:20:27.020
I don't think the network really understood
what we were.

01:20:27.020 --> 01:20:31.880
I think the audience was ahead of the network.

01:20:31.880 --> 01:20:39.210
On the other hand, there were certain I think
technical factors and historical factors that

01:20:39.210 --> 01:20:44.090
help Star Trek to become eventually the tremendous
syndication success that it was.

01:20:44.090 --> 01:20:51.300
In 1966 when we went on the air, we had not
yet put a man on the moon.

01:20:51.300 --> 01:20:54.080
We were cancelled in 1968 after 3 seasons.

01:20:54.080 --> 01:21:00.340
In 1969, you could walk out of doors of your
house 1 evening as I did and look up at that

01:21:00.340 --> 01:21:04.429
planet up there and know that there was a
human being standing on that planet for the

01:21:04.429 --> 01:21:05.429
first time.

01:21:05.429 --> 01:21:09.440
I think that really changed the American audience's
perception of science fiction.

01:21:09.440 --> 01:21:13.730
I think it affect the Star Trek a lot.

01:21:13.730 --> 01:21:18.020
The other major factor and it was technical
and perhaps we were more in touch with our

01:21:18.020 --> 01:21:25.010
time was around 1971, '72, the show was being
syndicated and that meant that local stations

01:21:25.010 --> 01:21:29.070
could put it on at the time of their choosing,
at the time that they felt that there was

01:21:29.070 --> 01:21:30.070
an audience for.

01:21:30.070 --> 01:21:32.370
When we were on the network, the stations
couldn't do that.

01:21:32.370 --> 01:21:37.300
They had to put it on the hour of the network
fed it and those hours were not well chosen.

01:21:37.300 --> 01:21:40.120
The network's taste for the show was not right.

01:21:40.120 --> 01:21:45.659
The very first step so they put on the air,
they chose a creature episode which we gave

01:21:45.659 --> 01:21:47.090
some sense of what they were hoping for.

01:21:47.090 --> 01:21:50.710
They were hoping for a monster show.

01:21:50.710 --> 01:21:53.639
The show was anything but a monster series.

01:21:53.639 --> 01:21:55.929
We're out of sync with them right from the
very start.

01:21:55.929 --> 01:21:59.330
I think eventually, the audience told the
stations that they wanted to see the show

01:21:59.330 --> 01:22:02.580
and when they want to see the show and you
could watch their show at Sunday afternoon

01:22:02.580 --> 01:22:09.440
at 3:00, Saturday morning at 9:00, every night
at 6:00, that kind of ... Suddenly, the audience

01:22:09.440 --> 01:22:10.440
was there.

01:22:10.440 --> 01:22:14.739
Michael: While we're providing the next question
with the microphone, just to make a brief

01:22:14.739 --> 01:22:19.040
comment about your question, you're absolutely
correct that the networks are losing total

01:22:19.040 --> 01:22:23.210
share of the audience and it's going to cable
into an independent stations into Fox but

01:22:23.210 --> 01:22:24.980
you also mentioned public television.

01:22:24.980 --> 01:22:27.429
Now, I work on public television.

01:22:27.429 --> 01:22:30.140
I host the show on public television.

01:22:30.140 --> 01:22:33.449
Unfortunately or fortunately depending on
your point of view, our audience is not increasing.

01:22:33.449 --> 01:22:39.160
In fact, I think people have suggested very
rightly that given the problems that Mr. Salman

01:22:39.160 --> 01:22:45.650
Rushdie has at the moment, if he wanted to
hide out where no one would find him, he should

01:22:45.650 --> 01:22:51.320
host a weekly series on PBS, he'd be safe.

01:22:51.320 --> 01:22:55.370
John: I'm John Sherwood.

01:22:55.370 --> 01:22:56.960
I'm with the Battle Creek Enquirer.

01:22:56.960 --> 01:23:02.860
In view of the fact that you had somewhat
criticized Mike Nichols for his failure to

01:23:02.860 --> 01:23:09.121
bring out a real social vision or what you
perceived as his social vision in Working

01:23:09.121 --> 01:23:15.210
Girl, I wonder what your perception is of
the duty of the individual artist be he director

01:23:15.210 --> 01:23:21.480
or actor to realize what he defines as a social
consciousness within him so that the audience

01:23:21.480 --> 01:23:22.650
perceives it.

01:23:22.650 --> 01:23:25.760
How far must that go with each project?

01:23:25.760 --> 01:23:31.540
Is that his responsibility to choose such
projects, for example?

01:23:31.540 --> 01:23:37.860
Leonard: Yes.

01:23:37.860 --> 01:23:48.449
Obviously, the ideal would be that every time
that person gets up the bat, he hits the ball

01:23:48.449 --> 01:23:49.449
well.

01:23:49.449 --> 01:23:50.449
It doesn't work that way in baseball.

01:23:50.449 --> 01:23:52.880
It's not going to work that way in the arts
either.

01:23:52.880 --> 01:23:55.850
Your greatest homerun hitter doesn't do it
every time.

01:23:55.850 --> 01:23:58.210
There is a question of percentages involved.

01:23:58.210 --> 01:24:01.030
I don't know what the origin of that project
was.

01:24:01.030 --> 01:24:06.230
I'm probably being unfair to Mr. Nichols for
sitting here speaking ill of his work.

01:24:06.230 --> 01:24:07.230
I admire.

01:24:07.230 --> 01:24:11.370
The problem is I admire him so much.

01:24:11.370 --> 01:24:17.090
Here's a film that's perfectly workable, perfectly
acceptable but I'm just ... I don't think

01:24:17.090 --> 01:24:22.900
it really gives us anything special or exciting
about Mike Nichols's view of the world.

01:24:22.900 --> 01:24:25.870
Michael: Which of his films would you say
do that?

01:24:25.870 --> 01:24:30.739
Leonard: Carnal Knowledge, for example and
so many others.

01:24:30.739 --> 01:24:32.889
Let's face it.

01:24:32.889 --> 01:24:34.370
This is a formula movie.

01:24:34.370 --> 01:24:41.139
This is a female Rocky comes from across the
river and comes into New York and makes it.

01:24:41.139 --> 01:24:43.070
I could have made it.

01:24:43.070 --> 01:24:48.610
Therefore, I say might do better because you're
a better film making than I am, more experienced,

01:24:48.610 --> 01:24:50.830
wiser, more sophisticated than I am.

01:24:50.830 --> 01:24:51.940
It's an anomaly.

01:24:51.940 --> 01:24:53.750
It's just one of those things.

01:24:53.750 --> 01:24:58.080
He'll probably come up with a brilliant film
next year and that would be that.

01:24:58.080 --> 01:25:02.810
He's done great work and I certainly don't
want to denigrate his career based on this

01:25:02.810 --> 01:25:03.810
successful movie.

01:25:03.810 --> 01:25:05.790
He's made a successful film.

01:25:05.790 --> 01:25:10.390
Speaker 11: I'd like to ask your opinion on
a subject that we've touched on a lot in the

01:25:10.390 --> 01:25:11.390
last 2 days.

01:25:11.390 --> 01:25:15.690
I'm going to use for an example, The Last
Temptation of Christ.

01:25:15.690 --> 01:25:23.139
This is a ... obviously offends a lot of people
but taking that totally not considering that

01:25:23.139 --> 01:25:26.500
at all the fact that it offends people, the
people that I've talked to who have seen the

01:25:26.500 --> 01:25:31.830
movie everyone of them have told me that it's
a totally boring movie in itself not to mention

01:25:31.830 --> 01:25:34.989
the fact that it did terrible in the Box Office.

01:25:34.989 --> 01:25:40.710
Now, what I want to know or is your opinion
on why does Hollywood do movies like this

01:25:40.710 --> 01:25:45.120
with ... They have to have some idea that
it's going to bomb when they're doing it.

01:25:45.120 --> 01:25:49.969
Leonard: I remember what I said before, the
Hollywood is not a person.

01:25:49.969 --> 01:25:51.940
Hollywood did not do this movie.

01:25:51.940 --> 01:25:55.489
A certain particular group of people said,
"Yes, we will give you the money."

01:25:55.489 --> 01:25:57.130
Others said no.

01:25:57.130 --> 01:25:59.719
That tells you immediately that Hollywood
is not a person.

01:25:59.719 --> 01:26:01.890
It is not Hollywood that did the movie.

01:26:01.890 --> 01:26:05.170
Some men, some place who had the authority
to say, "Yes, we'll do that movie," made a

01:26:05.170 --> 01:26:08.020
personal very human decision.

01:26:08.020 --> 01:26:12.980
That project was a Paramount and in pre-production
with Paramount.

01:26:12.980 --> 01:26:16.250
They decided against going forward with it
for whatever reason, they either they thought

01:26:16.250 --> 01:26:19.810
it was too controversial or too risky financially
or whatever.

01:26:19.810 --> 01:26:20.810
Somebody in Paramount said, "No.

01:26:20.810 --> 01:26:23.800
We will not go forward with this."

01:26:23.800 --> 01:26:31.810
Martin Scorsese had that project in hand and
begging for the money for some years and from

01:26:31.810 --> 01:26:32.810
a number of sources.

01:26:32.810 --> 01:26:34.460
It wasn't Hollywood that decided to make it.

01:26:34.460 --> 01:26:37.750
It was a human being on a given day made a
decision.

01:26:37.750 --> 01:26:43.730
I'm sure that that person felt a number of
things, that person or persons felt a number

01:26:43.730 --> 01:26:47.360
of things that Scorsese is a good film maker.

01:26:47.360 --> 01:26:54.159
Scorsese has a passion for this subject and
that passion will probably be reflected in

01:26:54.159 --> 01:26:55.159
the film.

01:26:55.159 --> 01:26:56.159
The title is a well-known title.

01:26:56.159 --> 01:26:57.910
It was a successful book.

01:26:57.910 --> 01:27:01.320
They may very well be an audience that wants
to see this film.

01:27:01.320 --> 01:27:05.340
Michael: I can add just a little bit to that
because I've done quite a bit of research

01:27:05.340 --> 01:27:10.400
about this film and how ... We know from having
heard me last night.

01:27:10.400 --> 01:27:13.280
The fact is that not only was that film at
Paramount.

01:27:13.280 --> 01:27:14.469
It was not only turned down in Paramount.

01:27:14.469 --> 01:27:19.440
It was also turned down at Warner's and Universal
greenlighted it, went ahead with the picture

01:27:19.440 --> 01:27:24.239
because Martin Scorsese was hot coming off
Color of Money which was quite a successful

01:27:24.239 --> 01:27:27.239
film and they made it as part of a package
deal.

01:27:27.239 --> 01:27:32.239
They wanted to get Scorsese's next 2 pictures
and as a condition of getting those pictures

01:27:32.239 --> 01:27:36.080
of Scorsese, he the director insisted, "Well
in that case, you got to give me $6 million

01:27:36.080 --> 01:27:38.780
and that's all they took to make Last Temptation."

01:27:38.780 --> 01:27:42.690
They did based upon the director and the idea
of getting this package with him.

01:27:42.690 --> 01:27:44.489
Leonard: Let me just say this.

01:27:44.489 --> 01:27:49.290
I can understand why some people would be
offended by that film and I can understand

01:27:49.290 --> 01:27:51.409
why some people would be bored by the film.

01:27:51.409 --> 01:27:55.480
I can understand other people saying that's
a great piece of work and the man should have

01:27:55.480 --> 01:27:56.790
the right to do it.

01:27:56.790 --> 01:28:02.239
What troubles me is people condemning something
that they have not had seen or read.

01:28:02.239 --> 01:28:03.840
That troubles me a lot.

01:28:03.840 --> 01:28:05.280
There was a lot of that on that film.

01:28:05.280 --> 01:28:09.440
People refusing to see it because they found
it offensive.

01:28:09.440 --> 01:28:10.440
I don't understand that.

01:28:10.440 --> 01:28:14.310
Michael: Mr. Nimoy, I should tell you that
last night when I spoke about the film, I

01:28:14.310 --> 01:28:18.239
asked if there was anyone in the audience
who had seen Last Temptation of Christ, only

01:28:18.239 --> 01:28:21.260
1 person raised his hand and he was wearing
a Roman collar.

01:28:21.260 --> 01:28:24.870
It was a priest who was here on campus.

01:28:24.870 --> 01:28:27.790
Thought that was an interesting ...
Leonard: Now understand me, if one chooses

01:28:27.790 --> 01:28:30.020
not to see it, that's one thing.

01:28:30.020 --> 01:28:33.800
If one chooses that others should not see
it because I have not seen it but find it

01:28:33.800 --> 01:28:34.800
offensive.

01:28:34.800 --> 01:28:35.800
That troubles me.

01:28:35.800 --> 01:28:42.420
Speaker 12: Rumor has it that Alfred Hitchcock
in directing the famous shower scene in Psycho,

01:28:42.420 --> 01:28:46.670
in order to get the facial expression that
he wanted from the actress, he made sure ... He

01:28:46.670 --> 01:28:50.630
couldn't get that facial expression that he
wanted and in order to do so, he made sure

01:28:50.630 --> 01:28:52.969
that when the water came out, it came out
ice cold.

01:28:52.969 --> 01:28:59.170
I was curious if you ever manipulated an actor
or actress or scene in the similar fashion.

01:28:59.170 --> 01:29:03.060
Leonard: He's a cold director, mean, mean
direc- No.

01:29:03.060 --> 01:29:09.159
I have ... I haven't done that kind of thing.

01:29:09.159 --> 01:29:10.159
Directors work in various ways.

01:29:10.159 --> 01:29:16.530
Hitchcock was never known to be an actor who
cared a lot about the ... A director who cared

01:29:16.530 --> 01:29:19.560
a lot about the art of acting.

01:29:19.560 --> 01:29:24.199
The actors for him were tools.

01:29:24.199 --> 01:29:29.960
They provided a service, a function that he
found necessary in order to make his film.

01:29:29.960 --> 01:29:40.610
I don't think he really had a serious or important
relationship with actors on artistic level.

01:29:40.610 --> 01:29:46.030
I'm very much in touch with actors coming
from the persuasion and having taught actors

01:29:46.030 --> 01:29:51.110
a lot and understanding what kind of vision
an actor can make given the opportunity.

01:29:51.110 --> 01:29:55.219
I'm very much in touch with opening up an
atmosphere which the actor feel safe to make

01:29:55.219 --> 01:29:57.680
a large investment and take chances.

01:29:57.680 --> 01:30:03.680
Speaker 13: Recently in Detroit, a young hockey
player named Bob Probert was arrested at the

01:30:03.680 --> 01:30:10.360
border for attempting to smuggle cocaine into
Canada and he's already been expelled from

01:30:10.360 --> 01:30:15.530
the National Hockey League and possibly will
never be able to play Hockey again.

01:30:15.530 --> 01:30:21.679
Do you think that the actors and actresses
in the screen guild which I think is a union

01:30:21.679 --> 01:30:27.380
for actors and actresses, do you think that
they should adopt similar very stringent standards

01:30:27.380 --> 01:30:32.180
because as we've all heard tonight, there
is such a strong impression of actors and

01:30:32.180 --> 01:30:36.900
actresses can make on us as a country and
wouldn't that be a wonderful statement to

01:30:36.900 --> 01:30:42.980
make our country if they would voluntarily
adopt standards that's stringent themselves?

01:30:42.980 --> 01:30:48.940
Michael: Good question.

01:30:48.940 --> 01:30:57.929
Leonard: The movie stars through the years
have come in a lot of different shapes and

01:30:57.929 --> 01:31:02.870
sizes morally and ethically as we know.

01:31:02.870 --> 01:31:12.110
In some cases for whatever reason, some stars
have found themselves in obscurity for having

01:31:12.110 --> 01:31:17.020
gotten involved in some questionable moral
situation or situations the audiences found

01:31:17.020 --> 01:31:19.900
questionable morally or ethically.

01:31:19.900 --> 01:31:26.170
Other actors and actresses have seemed to
thrive on it, flagrant about their personal

01:31:26.170 --> 01:31:30.270
lives and what their personal lives are about
and develop enormous followings.

01:31:30.270 --> 01:31:32.530
I don't think you can legislate that.

01:31:32.530 --> 01:31:41.010
What I find terribly painful is the growing
knowledge that athletes who participate in

01:31:41.010 --> 01:31:47.010
a foot race, which should be the most equal
kind of competition.

01:31:47.010 --> 01:31:48.989
The most basic kind of competition.

01:31:48.989 --> 01:31:57.100
One person running against another at given
distance have cheated.

01:31:57.100 --> 01:32:01.710
That I find so painful and I think is part
of this conversation we had earlier about

01:32:01.710 --> 01:32:05.010
our view of the world in ourselves these days.

01:32:05.010 --> 01:32:12.130
What can you believe in if there's an extra
meter or an extra foot or an extra leap available

01:32:12.130 --> 01:32:15.469
as a result of steroids and one is using them,
the other isn't.

01:32:15.469 --> 01:32:20.430
We suddenly discovered that the competition
wasn't real.

01:32:20.430 --> 01:32:28.889
It wasn't real and we invest so much emotion
in the effort of these people in their training

01:32:28.889 --> 01:32:35.110
and of the progress of their careers and in
the event, we make such an investment in the

01:32:35.110 --> 01:32:40.980
human effort to be the best watching a human
makes his effort to be the best.

01:32:40.980 --> 01:32:49.500
I find it so painful to discover that there's
kind of cheating going on.

01:32:49.500 --> 01:32:56.350
I think in athletics where there are people's
career's at stake where there's money at stake

01:32:56.350 --> 01:33:02.360
where there endorsements on ... Obviously,
I think some things got to be done to clean

01:33:02.360 --> 01:33:03.360
up that act.

01:33:03.360 --> 01:33:05.920
I don't know if you can do it with actors
and actresses.

01:33:05.920 --> 01:33:09.650
Michael: Let me be a pain in the neck and
do a followup on that.

01:33:09.650 --> 01:33:13.130
Given everything that you've said, given everything
that you've said, wouldn't you agree that

01:33:13.130 --> 01:33:17.620
actors particularly the very successful screen
actors are even more role models for people

01:33:17.620 --> 01:33:22.630
than athletes followed by even more millions
of people than hockey players and that if

01:33:22.630 --> 01:33:26.650
you're going to have this kind of standard
which you apparently support for people in

01:33:26.650 --> 01:33:32.960
athletics what about the star ... Let's take
and extreme example.

01:33:32.960 --> 01:33:38.231
Motion Picture actor of some success who was
arrested selling drugs to someone else, would

01:33:38.231 --> 01:33:43.179
you say that that person should be terminated
as a working actor?

01:33:43.179 --> 01:33:46.280
Leonard: I'm going to plead ignorance here.

01:33:46.280 --> 01:33:47.280
I don't know.

01:33:47.280 --> 01:33:49.580
Speaker 13: What studio?

01:33:49.580 --> 01:33:50.780
Is that…

01:33:50.780 --> 01:33:53.170
Michael: The point is ... The question was
very well asked.

01:33:53.170 --> 01:33:58.530
It is possible that SAG, Screen Actors Guild,
could impose a standard where he would lose

01:33:58.530 --> 01:33:59.949
his membership in the guild.

01:33:59.949 --> 01:34:04.050
Leonard: Then, the question becomes a legal
question, doesn't it?

01:34:04.050 --> 01:34:09.550
Does the Screen Actor Guild have the legal
right to do that and whoever the actor ...

01:34:09.550 --> 01:34:12.480
Michael: They can throw you out for breaking
a strike.

01:34:12.480 --> 01:34:16.940
Leonard: I think that's quite different, quite
different.

01:34:16.940 --> 01:34:20.630
You have broken the rules of the union and
there is a rule that says if you break that

01:34:20.630 --> 01:34:22.230
particular rule, you're going to be expelled.

01:34:22.230 --> 01:34:26.520
Michael: What if you have a rule in the union
that says it's against the rules…

01:34:26.520 --> 01:34:30.239
Leonard: Now, you start to legislate that
rule and you start to put that rule into effect

01:34:30.239 --> 01:34:31.570
and find out what the legalities are.

01:34:31.570 --> 01:34:32.945
Somebody's going to come along and say, "Well,
wait a minute.

01:34:32.945 --> 01:34:37.659
I'm not sure we can do that because ..." You're
going to have to work out the question legally

01:34:37.659 --> 01:34:42.870
and the question will be worked out legally
based on the consensus of opinion in our society

01:34:42.870 --> 01:34:44.210
today in terms of what we want.

01:34:44.210 --> 01:34:46.540
Do we want that rule, that new rule?

01:34:46.540 --> 01:34:47.980
Do we feel it’s necessary?

01:34:47.980 --> 01:34:50.060
Then it would become part of a society.

01:34:50.060 --> 01:34:53.650
I'm not sure that I could speak to the issue
of whether it's legal right now.

01:34:53.650 --> 01:34:54.840
That's why I have to plead ignorance here.

01:34:54.840 --> 01:34:58.409
Michael: If I can suggest to the questionnaire
and I'll shut up on this, you should ... We

01:34:58.409 --> 01:35:02.600
have a new drugs czar in this country named
William Benet and I think you should write

01:35:02.600 --> 01:35:04.830
to him with that suggestion.

01:35:04.830 --> 01:35:07.530
I think he would have a very interesting time
with that.

01:35:07.530 --> 01:35:13.840
Leonard: It's obviously a very provocative
question.

01:35:13.840 --> 01:35:22.350
I would remind you that there was a time when
a career of a luminous star lady was destroyed.

01:35:22.350 --> 01:35:25.560
Her career was destroyed because she had an
extra-marital affair.

01:35:25.560 --> 01:35:26.560
Michael: Ingrid Bergman.

01:35:26.560 --> 01:35:28.190
Leonard: That's right.

01:35:28.190 --> 01:35:32.780
Michael: The product of that affair is today
one of the leading stars in movies.

01:35:32.780 --> 01:35:33.780
Isabella Rossellini.

01:35:33.780 --> 01:35:34.850
Leonard: Isabella Rossellini.

01:35:34.850 --> 01:35:39.060
Ingrid Bergman was at the height of her career
when she had an extra-marital affair with

01:35:39.060 --> 01:35:42.600
Rossellini in Italy and she was out of the
movie business.

01:35:42.600 --> 01:35:43.840
Unusual, unhireable.

01:35:43.840 --> 01:35:46.110
Audiences refuse to see her.

01:35:46.110 --> 01:35:47.770
Times changed.

01:35:47.770 --> 01:35:52.800
Speaker 14: Mr. Nimoy, I'd like to change
the subject completely and address the issue

01:35:52.800 --> 01:35:56.800
of creativity in Hollywood and in society
in general.

01:35:56.800 --> 01:35:57.800
Leonard: Question?

01:35:57.800 --> 01:36:01.000
Speaker 14: It's going to be different.

01:36:01.000 --> 01:36:07.570
It seems to me the product that Hollywood
sells is not only entertainment but creativity

01:36:07.570 --> 01:36:14.670
and there's been a lot of discussion in recent
years about how TV has a negative effect on

01:36:14.670 --> 01:36:19.869
children, for instance, in society in general
because the viewer is viewing and observing

01:36:19.869 --> 01:36:26.110
as opposed to engaging in a creative and productive
activity themselves.

01:36:26.110 --> 01:36:35.840
I'm wondering what your view is of Hollywood
kind of being the electronic fireplace that

01:36:35.840 --> 01:36:40.880
your product is really being the devil's advocate
and negative one in a sense that it has a

01:36:40.880 --> 01:36:49.900
negative impact on people and I guess my question
is by sitting in a theater watching a movie

01:36:49.900 --> 01:36:55.010
or sitting in front of the TV and watching
TV, our people not engaging and being creative

01:36:55.010 --> 01:36:58.739
themselves and to Hollywood's credit ...
Leonard: You're absolutely right.

01:36:58.739 --> 01:36:59.739
I think ... Yeah.

01:36:59.739 --> 01:37:01.770
I would say you're absolutely right.

01:37:01.770 --> 01:37:05.989
There is nowhere near enough really creative
work being done.

01:37:05.989 --> 01:37:07.920
There is too much non-creative work being
done.

01:37:07.920 --> 01:37:10.570
There is too much kind of dumb stuff being
done.

01:37:10.570 --> 01:37:11.850
There are dumb television shows.

01:37:11.850 --> 01:37:15.050
There are dumb movies which are extraordinarily
successful.

01:37:15.050 --> 01:37:18.480
I agree with that 100%.

01:37:18.480 --> 01:37:23.199
On the other hand, the question is should
the kids be sitting in front of TV watching

01:37:23.199 --> 01:37:28.469
that and are they, because the parents find
easier to let them do that than to insist

01:37:28.469 --> 01:37:32.420
that the set is off during the week, you must
be involved in some other kind of creative

01:37:32.420 --> 01:37:33.990
or educational process.

01:37:33.990 --> 01:37:35.220
You cannot watch that tonight.

01:37:35.220 --> 01:37:39.370
Now that becomes a tug of war and battle in
the house which is a tough and painful battle

01:37:39.370 --> 01:37:40.469
I know that.

01:37:40.469 --> 01:37:42.930
I know that.

01:37:42.930 --> 01:37:47.440
It may be just easier to give in.

01:37:47.440 --> 01:37:52.710
If bad television programming exist, does
that mean Hollywood is it fault for not educating

01:37:52.710 --> 01:37:53.710
your children?

01:37:53.710 --> 01:37:57.810
Where is the responsibility for making the
choices?

01:37:57.810 --> 01:37:59.230
It's not easy.

01:37:59.230 --> 01:38:00.230
I know that.

01:38:00.230 --> 01:38:06.640
It's in the home and you got the set or sets
and the VCR or VCRs and the discs and all

01:38:06.640 --> 01:38:10.550
the rest of it, it's all there.

01:38:10.550 --> 01:38:15.150
Are we really doing a proper job in our homes
of saying you can only use that for this purpose

01:38:15.150 --> 01:38:19.179
and at this time and this other time must
be set aside for what you're talking about,

01:38:19.179 --> 01:38:20.860
the education and the creativity.

01:38:20.860 --> 01:38:24.659
Michael: You used a phrase and I'm just coming
out briefly, video fireplace.

01:38:24.659 --> 01:38:29.260
One of the most alarming things that I have
read and heard about recently is there was

01:38:29.260 --> 01:38:36.460
a best-selling video cassette called video
fireplace where it is a 120 minutes of a crackling

01:38:36.460 --> 01:38:42.000
fire in a fireplace and you plug it into your
VCR and there you area.

01:38:42.000 --> 01:38:45.840
Wherever you are in your condo or your apartment,
you have a fire.

01:38:45.840 --> 01:38:49.810
This is the extent to which we become dependent
on this machine.

01:38:49.810 --> 01:38:51.130
Run out to your stores and get it.

01:38:51.130 --> 01:38:56.460
You can also get video dog and you can also
get video baby which has a little baby crawling

01:38:56.460 --> 01:39:01.730
around and going gugu cuckoo but not doing
anything nasty.

01:39:01.730 --> 01:39:09.420
Speaker 15: Mr. Nimoy, I want to change the
subject even further than she just did.

01:39:09.420 --> 01:39:11.989
I remember you did a series called In Search
Of.

01:39:11.989 --> 01:39:13.440
I was just wondering whatever happened to
that?

01:39:13.440 --> 01:39:17.700
Leonard: Whatever happened to it was we did
7 years of it.

01:39:17.700 --> 01:39:22.760
We did about 160 or 170 episodes.

01:39:22.760 --> 01:39:32.640
It was extraordinarily successful television
series sometimes informative, sometimes silly,

01:39:32.640 --> 01:39:37.300
sometimes hokey.

01:39:37.300 --> 01:39:43.790
I was thinking that I … knowing that I was
coming here about an interesting phenomenon

01:39:43.790 --> 01:39:46.361
that I heard about while I was doing that
series.

01:39:46.361 --> 01:39:52.540
There was a scientist named Allen Hynek who
was part of the Air Force Blue Book Commission

01:39:52.540 --> 01:40:02.449
assigned to investigating UFOs, supposedly
the scientific inquiry into the question.

01:40:02.449 --> 01:40:11.440
Allen Hynek when the Blue Book was published
went out ... came out with a book of his own

01:40:11.440 --> 01:40:15.390
which disproved the existence of UFOs.

01:40:15.390 --> 01:40:22.469
He went out of the road to publicize the book.

01:40:22.469 --> 01:40:26.130
During the course of his trip around the country,
you got a call from his publisher and he told

01:40:26.130 --> 01:40:27.920
me the story.

01:40:27.920 --> 01:40:30.350
His publisher said, "The book is not selling.

01:40:30.350 --> 01:40:32.600
We have a problem.

01:40:32.600 --> 01:40:37.000
The problem is that people don't want to hear
the UFOs don't exist.

01:40:37.000 --> 01:40:42.020
People are much more interested in the possibility
that they do exist."

01:40:42.020 --> 01:40:48.250
Is it possible that in your public appearances
on the subject of this book you're going to

01:40:48.250 --> 01:40:54.010
leave some room for the possibility that UFOs
do exist?

01:40:54.010 --> 01:40:58.650
In Search Of was an extremely successful show
which left that possibility open.

01:40:58.650 --> 01:41:04.100
We discovered that the audience really did
not want to have any of that stuff disproved.

01:41:04.100 --> 01:41:07.700
What they wanted was stories from people who
claimed of seeing UFOs.

01:41:07.700 --> 01:41:11.730
They want a discussion about why people think
UFOs exist and all that sort of thing.

01:41:11.730 --> 01:41:14.063
We always take pains to say we don't have
any physical evidence that they exist.

01:41:14.063 --> 01:41:16.429
Here's a person who claims he was on one.

01:41:16.429 --> 01:41:18.030
Let's talk to him.

01:41:18.030 --> 01:41:21.600
I had a good time on that show.

01:41:21.600 --> 01:41:25.600
Michael: I think you'll be delighted to know
and this is no joke that Big Foot has recently

01:41:25.600 --> 01:41:29.179
been sighted here in Hillsdale, Michigan.

01:41:29.179 --> 01:41:31.610
Leonard: We did a show about Big Foot.

01:41:31.610 --> 01:41:36.770
Now, I'm delighted to discover that in some
counties in Washington State, it's illegal

01:41:36.770 --> 01:41:38.820
to shoot Big Foot.

01:41:38.820 --> 01:41:42.639
Michael: With the camera.

01:41:42.639 --> 01:41:45.220
Denise: Hello, Mr. Nimoy.

01:41:45.220 --> 01:41:46.360
My name is Denise Walton.

01:41:46.360 --> 01:41:51.580
I'm a sophomore here and I understand that
this will be the last question for the evening.

01:41:51.580 --> 01:41:56.489
My question stems from some studying I've
been doing in an English literature class.

01:41:56.489 --> 01:42:01.740
Recently, my English literature class read
words with poem entitled Michael.

01:42:01.740 --> 01:42:09.090
The fulcrum of that poem was the building
between a father and a son of a pile of rocks

01:42:09.090 --> 01:42:17.670
which would be a sheep trap when the sun returned
from the city in an attempt to save the family

01:42:17.670 --> 01:42:21.180
from financial ruin.

01:42:21.180 --> 01:42:25.570
This pile was a sacred covenant between the
father and son.

01:42:25.570 --> 01:42:31.860
To simplify the poem, subsequently what happens
is this covenant was broken.

01:42:31.860 --> 01:42:36.680
The experience of reading this poem was at
in the future, whenever I'll walk through

01:42:36.680 --> 01:42:41.160
the woods, I guess I'll never look the same
way at a pile of rocks.

01:42:41.160 --> 01:42:48.140
Earlier, during the course of the interview,
you alluded that your work with The Good Mother

01:42:48.140 --> 01:42:53.030
puts you in touch with your own sense of loss.

01:42:53.030 --> 01:42:55.310
My question is two-fold.

01:42:55.310 --> 01:43:01.340
What experience of a role as an actor or a
director has the biggest impact on your personal

01:43:01.340 --> 01:43:02.340
life?

01:43:02.340 --> 01:43:06.889
It touched you or changed you so if you never
perceived in a situation the same way and

01:43:06.889 --> 01:43:12.960
also, what experience of a role as an actor
or a director had you hoped would have an

01:43:12.960 --> 01:43:18.440
impact on society?

01:43:18.440 --> 01:43:28.389
Leonard: Thought-provoking.

01:43:28.389 --> 01:43:38.890
I've had some extraordinary experiences as
both actor and director but I think I would

01:43:38.890 --> 01:43:48.500
be evasive if I didn't talk about the Spock
experience because of all the roles that I've

01:43:48.500 --> 01:43:52.100
played, all of the jobs that I've had as actor
or director obviously has had the most profound

01:43:52.100 --> 01:43:56.600
effect on both my life and career.

01:43:56.600 --> 01:44:05.040
The longest lasting effect and very dramatic
the changes that I've experienced as a result

01:44:05.040 --> 01:44:06.040
of it.

01:44:06.040 --> 01:44:14.460
It changed me psychologically playing the
character, changed me psychologically.

01:44:14.460 --> 01:44:21.630
Certainly changed my lifestyle a lot, very
simply it was a first steady job I ever had

01:44:21.630 --> 01:44:23.940
in my business.

01:44:23.940 --> 01:44:31.179
I have been making up a living as an actor
and as a teacher for some years before Star

01:44:31.179 --> 01:44:33.390
Trek came along in 1966.

01:44:33.390 --> 01:44:37.130
I have never had a job that lasted longer
than 2 weeks.

01:44:37.130 --> 01:44:40.410
I was a freelance actor meaning that you job
around.

01:44:40.410 --> 01:44:41.920
You go from job to job.

01:44:41.920 --> 01:44:48.370
I had never had a job where my name was painted
on the parking space.

01:44:48.370 --> 01:44:53.440
In most cases, it's put on with chalk if at
all.

01:44:53.440 --> 01:45:00.650
I starred in a movie in 1951 a little B movie
that was made in 9 days and my dressing room

01:45:00.650 --> 01:45:02.940
had Jane Nigh's name on a door.

01:45:02.940 --> 01:45:08.080
You don't even remember who Jane Nigh was.

01:45:08.080 --> 01:45:14.000
It had a profound effect on me in a lot of
ways and still does.

01:45:14.000 --> 01:45:16.210
Still does.

01:45:16.210 --> 01:45:21.410
Coming to the point of this evening, the impact
on a society I think was enormous.

01:45:21.410 --> 01:45:31.850
I still constantly come up across people who
tell me how much they were affected by the

01:45:31.850 --> 01:45:34.530
series or the character or both.

01:45:34.530 --> 01:45:39.900
It's a very proud thing because I think the
effect essentially had been positive.

01:45:39.900 --> 01:45:47.429
It is sometimes still overwhelming when I
see ... Last night, I was watching 60 Minutes

01:45:47.429 --> 01:45:54.150
and there was a segment on television over
the years.

01:45:54.150 --> 01:46:01.350
Here were these very brief flashes of historical
events in television, a historical character

01:46:01.350 --> 01:46:07.190
in television and there was Spock, my old
buddy like a bang on 60 Minutes.

01:46:07.190 --> 01:46:12.679
You understand who that is and people understand
where that character fits in the culture and

01:46:12.679 --> 01:46:15.530
what's that all about.

01:46:15.530 --> 01:46:22.159
You see Spock for president bumper stickers
during presidential campaigns and people understand

01:46:22.159 --> 01:46:26.760
that and beam me up Scotty there's no intelligent
life down here that sort of thing.

01:46:26.760 --> 01:46:27.760
We understand that.

01:46:27.760 --> 01:46:28.760
It's in the culture.

01:46:28.760 --> 01:46:33.010
It is in the culture and not necessarily in
a bad way.

01:46:33.010 --> 01:46:38.290
I think a lot of it is fun, a lot of it is
very healthy because it stands the ... The

01:46:38.290 --> 01:46:45.909
character stands for I think the dignity for
self realization, for achievement, for intelligence,

01:46:45.909 --> 01:46:49.320
logic, compassion, a lot of good things.

01:46:49.320 --> 01:46:58.070
It's had an enormously profound effect on
my life and I'm sure it will indefinitely.

01:46:58.070 --> 01:47:03.830
Michael: To conclude, to take advantage of
my position here, I want to first of all thank

01:47:03.830 --> 01:47:08.969
all of the questionnaires as I sort of indicated
to you privately, this is an extraordinary

01:47:08.969 --> 01:47:10.980
group at Hillsdale.

01:47:10.980 --> 01:47:18.429
The questionnaires are people who dared boldly
go where no questionnaires had gone before,

01:47:18.429 --> 01:47:22.280
and obviously not only an extraordinary group
of questionnaires but an extraordinary guest

01:47:22.280 --> 01:47:26.980
in the presence of Leonard Nimoy ... in the
person of Leonard Nimoy and speaking as a

01:47:26.980 --> 01:47:31.219
friend of Hillsdale though not quite a member
of the family, I'm sure that I speak for everyone

01:47:31.219 --> 01:47:36.469
here and expressing thanks to you and the
hope that this will by no means be your last

01:47:36.469 --> 01:47:38.179
visit to this part of our planet.

01:47:38.179 --> 01:47:39.250
Leonard: Thank you very much.

